"""dpkg-stereo: Lean Code for installed packages (Sparky Stereo OS Lean Code policy, "Install-time
projection: dpkg-stereo", Daniel 2026-10-09).

Third-party .deb files stay as their distributions built them. Our lc1-marked builds arrive already
projected and are skipped after their manifests are checked. After dpkg installs unmarked packages,
dpkg-stereo projects the text files they installed into their machine form (MR, the removable comments
absent) through the qualified adapters of the lean package, checks every projection with the file's
consumer, and records it. Before dpkg runs, every projected conffile whose bytes still equal its lean
form is put back to its pristine bytes, so dpkg sees an unmodified conffile and never asks; a conffile
the person edited is left alone and dpkg's usual rules apply. Everything is recorded and reversible.

usage: dpkg-stereo [--root DIR] [--map /etc=DIR ...] COMMAND
  --project [PACKAGE...]   project these packages' files (all installed packages without a name)
  --pre-invoke             before dpkg (dpkg's pre-invoke hook): projected conffiles back to pristine
  --post-invoke            after dpkg (dpkg's post-invoke hook): project what changed, reproject conffiles
  --verify [PACKAGE...]    projected files against their lean hashes, pristine records against dpkg's
  --restore [PACKAGE...]   the original files back (all packages without a name), records dropped; a package
                           version neither APT's cache nor its sources hold comes from snapshot.debian.org when
                           it is Debian's, and what cannot come back is said with the one thing that brings it back
  --restore [PACKAGE...] --deb FILE.deb ...  the same, with these .deb files as the originals: each is taken
                           only when it is exactly an installed version and every file it gives back is the
                           recorded original (without a PACKAGE, the files' own packages are restored)
  --repair-consumers       restore projections whose old consumer check was withdrawn, and project
                           again the C and C++ files an older renderer gave another form (#line
                           directives, or every authored line kept); those whose .deb is nowhere wait,
                           and this tries them again now
  --hold PACKAGE|PATH      never project it again (restores it first); --unhold undoes that
  --hr PATH                write the full HR copy of an installed file to /usr/share/doc/PKG/hr/PATH
  --fetch-hr               write the HR copies of every projected setting (after the first online update)
  --resolve PATH:LINE[:COL]  an error location in a lean file -> the authored line, found by the line's
                           content in the authored file (the neighbouring lines pick among equal ones)
  --resolve-render PATH:LINE[:COL]  the same through the render's own line map: the authored file is
                           rendered again, and a lean file that is not that render is refused
  --manifest FILE          the coverage manifest of every comment-bearing text file, with reasons
  --status                 what is projected, held, and left alone, and how many problems are kept
  --problems               the kept problems: what did not complete, why, and what repairs it (exit 1 while any)
  --repair                 run again what each kept problem names; what succeeds is cleared
  --dismiss STEP           hide one kept problem for you (it comes back if the step fails again)
  --notify                 start the session's notifier, which shows every kept problem
Options: --jobs N (parallel checks, default 4), --image (building an image), --if-changed (with
--repair-consumers: try a waiting C or C++ file again only when its package, APT's lists or APT's cache
changed since the last try, as the dpkg hooks do; the postinst uses it).
Environment: DPKG_STEREO_DISABLE=1 makes the dpkg hooks do nothing (an image build that projects in its
own step); DPKG_STEREO_NO_PROJECT=1 skips the first projection when the package is installed.

Nothing fails silently (Daniel, 2026-11-01: "either it works or not and the user must know"). A step that cannot
do what it is for says what failed, why and what to do, in the system's language with the English line beside it,
and the edition's one engine (sparky-stereo-problems) keeps it in /var/lib/sparky-stereo-problems until the step
next succeeds or --repair fixes it. dpkg's
hooks still return 0, because dpkg stops its run when a hook fails ("cannot execute hook ... exit code"), and APT's
update hook too, because APT then fails the whole update ("Problem executing scripts APT::Update::Post-Invoke-
Success"); every file stays its package's original or a checked projection either way. --verify --deep that could
not compare a file with its package's .deb says so and exits 2, never clean.
"""

import fcntl
import io
import os
import stat
import sys
import time

sys.path.insert(0, os.environ.get('DPKG_STEREO_LIB', '/usr/lib/dpkg-stereo'))


class _Lazy:
    """A module imported the first time it is needed. dpkg's hooks usually need none of these, and importing them all
    cost each hook more than its own work did (ORDER 367: about 64 ms of a 111 ms hook that did nothing, Python 3.14
    on a Ryzen 5 5600G; concurrent.futures alone brings logging, traceback and _colorize)."""
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name
        self.mod = None

    def __getattr__(self, attr):
        if self.mod is None:
            import importlib
            self.mod = importlib.import_module(self.name)
        return getattr(self.mod, attr)


project = _Lazy('lean.project')
locate = _Lazy('lean.locate')
consumers = _Lazy('lean.consumers')
lean_pkg = _Lazy('lean')
lc_package = _Lazy('lean.package')
argparse = _Lazy('argparse')
bisect = _Lazy('bisect')
collections = _Lazy('collections')
json = _Lazy('json')
lzma = _Lazy('lzma')
re = _Lazy('re')
shutil = _Lazy('shutil')
subprocess = _Lazy('subprocess')
tarfile = _Lazy('tarfile')
tempfile = _Lazy('tempfile')
urllib_parse = _Lazy('urllib.parse')
# SHA-256 and MD5 come from OpenSSL's binding, which takes about 3 ms to load, and a run that changes nothing reads no
# file to hash; BLAKE2, which says whether dpkg's database changed, is CPython's own (Lib/hashlib.py takes blake2b
# from _blake2, never from OpenSSL)
hashlib = _Lazy('hashlib')
try:
    from _blake2 import blake2b
except ImportError:
    from hashlib import blake2b

STATE = '/var/lib/dpkg-stereo'
FILES = 'files.tsv'
LEFT = 'left.tsv'
HOLD = 'hold'
PKGS = 'packages.tsv'
CONFFILES = 'conffiles.tsv'
PRISTINE = 'pristine'
FILE_COLS = ('path', 'package', 'version', 'conffile', 'sha_orig', 'md5_orig', 'sha_lean', 'handler', 'variant',
             'bytes_orig', 'bytes_lean')
PKG_COLS = ('package', 'version', 'list_mtime', 'written')
TEXT_SIZE_MAX = 4 << 20
PERSON_CHANGED = 'conffile changed by the person (dpkg rules apply, never projected)'

# What failed, why and what to do, kept until it is repaired (0.1.25). The messages are the package's language files
# (LOCALn="text", %s for the arguments), in the nine languages the edition requires; the English line goes beside
# any other language, for support.
PROBLEMS = 'problems'
LANG_DIR = os.environ.get('DPKG_STEREO_LANG_DIR', '/usr/share/sparky/dpkg-stereo')
LANGS = ('en', 'pl', 'pt_BR', 'fr', 'de', 'ja', 'ko', 'zh_CN', 'zh_TW')
TAG = 'dpkg-stereo'
REPAIR = 'sudo dpkg-stereo --repair'
# the step's name in the messages; what --repair runs again for it
STEPS = {'post-invoke': 'LOCAL1', 'pre-invoke': 'LOCAL2', 'project': 'LOCAL3', 'restore': 'LOCAL4',
         'repair-consumers': 'LOCAL5', 'fetch-hr': 'LOCAL6', 'bytecode': 'LOCAL7'}
OUTPUT_LINES = 200
# snapshot.debian.org, the Debian Project's archive of every package version Debian has published, and its
# machine-readable interface: /mr/binary/NAME/VERSION/binfiles?fileinfo=1 names each architecture's .deb by its
# SHA-1, /file/SHA1 is that file (ORDER 365). DPKG_STEREO_SNAPSHOT names another server of the same interface (the
# tests' local one).
SNAPSHOT = os.environ.get('DPKG_STEREO_SNAPSHOT', 'https://snapshot.debian.org').rstrip('/')
APT_HELPER = '/usr/lib/apt/apt-helper'
# why an original stays lean, and what brings it back: the messages of the language files (restore, ORDER 365)
WHY = {'unreachable': 'LOCAL26', 'not-on-snapshot': 'LOCAL27', 'other-archive': 'LOCAL28', 'refused': 'LOCAL29',
       'other-bytes': 'LOCAL30', 'apt': 'LOCAL31', 'unreadable': 'LOCAL32', 'root': 'LOCAL33', 'given': 'LOCAL34',
       'unlisted': 'LOCAL50'}


def language(P=None):
    """The system's language among the nine: LANG, else /etc/default/locale; the whole name, then the bare language,
    then English; Chinese keeps its territory, zh_CN when none is named."""
    lang = os.environ.get('LANG', '')
    if not lang or lang in ('C', 'POSIX') or lang.startswith('C.'):
        try:
            with open((P.real('/etc/default/locale') if P else '/etc/default/locale'), encoding='utf-8') as f:
                for line in f:
                    if line.startswith('LANG='):
                        lang = line.split('=', 1)[1].strip().strip('"')
        except OSError:
            pass
    lang = lang.split('.')[0].split('@')[0]
    for code in ([lang, 'zh_CN'] if lang.startswith('zh') else [lang, lang.split('_')[0]]):
        if code in LANGS:
            return code
    return 'en'


_strings = {}


def strings(lang):
    if lang not in _strings:
        values = {}
        try:
            with open(os.path.join(LANG_DIR, lang), encoding='utf-8') as f:
                for line in f:
                    line = line.strip()
                    if '=' in line and not line.startswith('#'):
                        key, value = line.split('=', 1)
                        values[key.strip()] = value.strip().strip('"')
        except OSError:
            pass
        _strings[lang] = values
    return _strings[lang]


def text(key, args=(), lang=None):
    """KEY in LANG (the system's language when None), its %s filled with ARGS; an argument [KEY, ARG...] is that
    message itself, and an argument @KEY that message without arguments, as the problems engine renders them. A key
    missing from the language's file is taken from the English file, and a key missing from both is printed with its
    arguments, never left out."""
    lang = lang or language()
    template = strings(lang).get(key) or strings('en').get(key)

    def fill(a):
        if isinstance(a, (list, tuple)) and a:
            return text(a[0], a[1:], lang)
        if isinstance(a, str) and len(a) > 1 and a[0] == '@' and (strings(lang).get(a[1:]) or strings('en').get(a[1:])):
            return text(a[1:], (), lang)
        return str(a)
    filled = [fill(a) for a in args]
    if template is None:
        return ' '.join([key] + filled)
    try:
        return template % tuple(filled)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return ' '.join([template] + filled)


def say(key, args=(), stream=None):
    """KEY on standard error (or STREAM) in the system's language, and in English too when that is another."""
    stream = stream or sys.stderr
    lang = language()
    print(f'{TAG}: {text(key, args, lang)}', file=stream, flush=True)
    if lang != 'en':
        print(f'{TAG}: (en) {text(key, args, "en")}', file=stream, flush=True)


def engine(P):
    """The edition's one engine (sparky-stereo-problems, ORDER 350), over P's root; None, said, when it cannot load."""
    os.environ['SPARKY_STEREO_ROOT'] = P.root
    try:
        import sparky_stereo_problems
        return sparky_stereo_problems
    except (ImportError, OSError) as error:
        print(f'{TAG}: the problems engine cannot be loaded ({error}); what failed is said here only', file=sys.stderr,
              flush=True)
        return None


def problems_store(P):
    return P.root + '/var/lib/sparky-stereo-problems'


def keep_problem(P, step, reason, detail='', repair=REPAIR, items=None, quiet=False):
    """Says (unless QUIET) and keeps that STEP did not complete, with REASON (one line, or [KEY, ARG...] for a
    message of the language files), DETAIL (the evidence: the end of a traceback or of a command's output), ITEMS
    (what it could not do, for --repair) and the REPAIR command, in the edition's one engine; its notifier shows it,
    and its Repair runs dpkg-stereo --repair."""
    if not quiet:
        say('LOCAL8', [[STEPS[step]], reason])
        say('LOCAL9', [problems_store(P)])
        say('LOCAL10', [repair])
    e = engine(P)
    if e is None:
        return False
    try:
        e.keep(TAG, step, 'LOCAL8', [[STEPS[step]], reason], scope=e.MACHINE, output=detail[-20000:] or None,
               repair=[['dpkg-stereo', '--repair']], data={'items': items or [], 'repair': repair})
    except e.Failed as error:
        # the record is the evidence the repair needs: that it could not be kept is said too
        print(f'{TAG}: {error}', file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
        if error.english != str(error):
            print(f'{TAG}: (en) {error.english}', file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
        return False
    return True


def clear_problem(P, step):
    """STEP succeeded: its kept problem goes. The engine keeps one record per file, PACKAGE_KEY.json in the machine's
    store (its README, "Where problems are kept"), and its clear returns at once, doing nothing, when that file is not
    there (src/engine.zig, clear and readRecord); so when it is not there the engine is not loaded (loading it took
    about 6 ms of every dpkg hook, ORDER 367). Under pkexec the engine reads only the real root's store."""
    if all(c.isascii() and (c.isalnum() or c in '_@:+-') for c in step):
        root = '' if os.geteuid() == 0 and os.environ.get('PKEXEC_UID') is not None else P.root
        try:
            os.lstat(f'{root}/var/lib/sparky-stereo-problems/{TAG}_{step}.json')
        except FileNotFoundError:
            return True
        except OSError:
            pass
    e = engine(P)
    if e is None:
        return False
    try:
        e.clear(TAG, step, scope=e.MACHINE)
    except e.Failed as error:
        print(f'{TAG}: {error}', file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
        return False
    return True


def kept_problems(P):
    """The kept records, oldest first, as the engine lists them; an unreadable one is shown as unreadable, never
    skipped."""
    e = engine(P)
    if e is None:
        return []
    found = []
    for p in e.list(TAG):
        if 'unreadable' in p:
            found.append({'step': '-', 'time': '', 'unreadable': f'{p["file"]}: {p["unreadable"]}', 'file': p['file']})
            continue
        data = p.get('data') or {}
        found.append({'step': p['key'], 'time': p['first'], 'text': p['text_en'], 'key': p['message'], 'args': p['args'],
                      'detail': p['output'], 'items': data.get('items', []), 'repair': data.get('repair', REPAIR),
                      'file': p['file'], 'hidden': p['hidden']})
    return found


def problem_text(entry, lang=None):
    if entry.get('unreadable'):
        return entry['unreadable']
    return text(entry.get('key', ''), entry.get('args', []), lang)


# the steps of this run that kept a problem; the command then exits 1
FAILED_STEPS = set()


def error_line(error):
    return f'{type(error).__name__}: {error}'.split('\n')[0]


def error_detail():
    import traceback
    return ''.join(collections.deque(traceback.format_exc().splitlines(True), maxlen=OUTPUT_LINES))


class Paths:
    def __init__(self, root='/', maps=()):
        self.root = root.rstrip('/') or ''
        self.maps = sorted(((p.rstrip('/'), d.rstrip('/')) for p, d in maps), key=lambda x: -len(x[0]))

    def real(self, path):
        for prefix, d in self.maps:
            if path == prefix or path.startswith(prefix + '/'):
                return d + path[len(prefix):]
        return self.root + path

    def state(self, *parts):
        return os.path.join(self.real(STATE), *parts)


def sha256(b):
    return hashlib.sha256(b).hexdigest()


def md5(b):
    return hashlib.md5(b).hexdigest()


def read_table(path, cols):
    rows = {}
    try:
        with open(path, encoding='utf-8', errors='surrogateescape') as f:
            for line in f:
                if line.startswith('#') or not line.strip():
                    continue
                v = line.rstrip('\n').split('\t')
                rows[v[0]] = dict(zip(cols, v))
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass
    return rows


LEFT_COLS = ('path', 'package', 'handler', 'reason')


class Records(dict):
    """path -> record, kept per package (records/<package>.tsv, left/<package>.tsv), so a dpkg run reads and
    writes only the packages it touched. Loads every package, or only `pkgs`; `save()` writes the packages
    whose rows changed, and for projections the small conffile index pre-invoke reads."""

    def __init__(self, P, kind='records', pkgs=None):
        super().__init__()
        self.P, self.kind = P, kind
        self.cols = FILE_COLS if kind == 'records' else LEFT_COLS
        self.dir = P.state(kind)
        self.dirty = set()
        self.loaded = set()
        _migrate(P)
        if pkgs is None:
            try:
                names = [f[:-4] for f in os.listdir(self.dir) if f.endswith('.tsv')]
            except FileNotFoundError:
                names = []
        else:
            names = list(pkgs)
        for n in names:
            self.load(n)

    def load(self, pkg):
        if pkg in self.loaded:
            return
        self.loaded.add(pkg)
        dict.update(self, read_table(os.path.join(self.dir, pkg + '.tsv'), self.cols))

    def __setitem__(self, path, rec):
        self.load(rec['package'])
        old = self.get(path)
        if old and old['package'] != rec['package']:
            self.dirty.add(old['package'])
        self.dirty.add(rec['package'])
        dict.__setitem__(self, path, rec)

    def __delitem__(self, path):
        self.dirty.add(self[path]['package'])
        dict.__delitem__(self, path)

    def pop(self, path, *default):
        if path in self:
            self.dirty.add(self[path]['package'])
        return dict.pop(self, path, *default)

    def save(self):
        if not self.dirty:
            return
        os.makedirs(self.dir, exist_ok=True)
        by = {}
        for path, rec in self.items():
            if rec['package'] in self.dirty:
                by.setdefault(rec['package'], {})[path] = rec
        for pkg in self.dirty:
            f = os.path.join(self.dir, pkg + '.tsv')
            if by.get(pkg):
                write_table(f, self.cols, by[pkg])
            elif os.path.exists(f):
                os.unlink(f)
        if self.kind == 'records':
            idx = read_table(self.P.state(CONFFILES), FILE_COLS)
            idx = {k: v for k, v in idx.items() if v['package'] not in self.dirty}
            for pkg in self.dirty:
                idx.update({k: v for k, v in by.get(pkg, {}).items() if str(v.get('conffile')) == '1'})
            write_table(self.P.state(CONFFILES), FILE_COLS, idx)
        self.dirty = set()


def _migrate(P):
    """0.1.1 and 0.1.2 kept every record in one files.tsv and left.tsv: split them per package, once."""
    for old, kind, cols in ((FILES, 'records', FILE_COLS), (LEFT, 'left', LEFT_COLS)):
        f = P.state(old)
        if not os.path.exists(f):
            continue
        rows = read_table(f, cols)
        os.makedirs(P.state(kind), exist_ok=True)
        by = {}
        for path, rec in rows.items():
            by.setdefault(rec['package'], {})[path] = rec
        for pkg, r in by.items():
            write_table(os.path.join(P.state(kind), pkg + '.tsv'), cols, r)
        if kind == 'records':
            write_table(P.state(CONFFILES), FILE_COLS, {k: v for k, v in rows.items() if str(v.get('conffile')) == '1'})
        os.unlink(f)


def write_table(path, cols, rows):
    tmp = path + '.new'
    with open(tmp, 'w', encoding='utf-8', errors='surrogateescape') as f:
        f.write('#' + '\t'.join(cols) + '\n')
        for k in sorted(rows):
            f.write('\t'.join(str(rows[k].get(c, '')) for c in cols) + '\n')
    os.replace(tmp, path)


DB_FIELDS = ('Package', 'Version', 'Architecture', 'Status', 'Multi-Arch', 'Maintainer')


def conffile_entry(line):
    """One line of a Conffiles field read the way dpkg reads it (lib/dpkg/fields.c, f_conffiles): from the end
    of the line, the flag remove-on-upgrade, then the flag obsolete, then the hash, and the path is what is
    left, spaces included. (path, hash, flags), or None for a line without a path and a hash."""
    rest, flags = line.strip(), set()
    for flag in ('remove-on-upgrade', 'obsolete'):
        words = rest.rsplit(None, 1)
        if len(words) == 2 and words[1] == flag:
            flags.add(flag)
            rest = words[0]
    words = rest.rsplit(None, 1)
    return (words[0], words[1], flags) if len(words) == 2 else None


def _db_stanzas(f):
    """The fields dpkg-stereo reads from each stanza of a dpkg database file, Conffiles as {path: hash}.
    A remove-on-upgrade entry is left out: it names a conffile the package no longer ships (deb-conffiles(5):
    "These files must not exist in the binary package"), and its hash is the word newconffile when the
    package was installed without ever shipping the file (binutils-common's /etc/gprofng.rc, ORDER 325)."""
    rec, conf = {}, None
    for line in f:
        if line == '\n':
            if rec:
                yield rec
            rec, conf = {}, None
            continue
        if line[0] in ' \t':
            if conf is not None:
                entry = conffile_entry(line)
                # a path the package's conffiles list twice has two entries, and only the first one is dpkg's:
                # its lookups stop at the first match (src/main/unpack.c), and configuring skips the second, whose
                # .dpkg-new the first one used, so it keeps "newconffile" or an older version's hash
                # (src/main/configure.c; libcufile-13-4's /etc/ld.so.conf.d/gds-13-4.conf, nis's /etc/default/nis)
                if entry and 'remove-on-upgrade' not in entry[2]:
                    conf.setdefault(entry[0], entry[1])
            continue
        conf = None
        key, _, value = line.partition(':')
        if key in DB_FIELDS:
            rec[key] = value.strip()
        elif key == 'Conffiles':
            conf = rec['Conffiles'] = {}
    if rec:
        yield rec


def dpkg_db_files(P):
    """The files of dpkg's database in the order dpkg reads them, [(path, bytes)]: the status file, then every entry of
    its journal, /var/lib/dpkg/updates. A file that is not there is left out."""
    names = [P.real('/var/lib/dpkg/status')]
    updates = P.real('/var/lib/dpkg/updates')
    try:
        # dpkg reads only the names made of digits, all of one length, in sorted order (lib/dpkg/dbmodify.c)
        names += [os.path.join(updates, n) for n in sorted(os.listdir(updates)) if n.isascii() and n.isdigit()]
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass
    files = []
    for name in names:
        try:
            with open(name, 'rb') as f:
                files.append((name, f.read()))
        except FileNotFoundError:
            pass
    return files


def db_digest(files):
    """What dpkg's database holds, as one short digest of the bytes of its files (dpkg_db_files). dpkg writes the status
    file again at the end of every run that opens it for writing, a run that changed nothing included
    (lib/dpkg/dbmodify.c, modstatdb_shutdown and modstatdb_checkpoint), so its time says nothing; its bytes say whether
    any package's version or state changed."""
    h = blake2b(digest_size=16)
    for name, data in files:
        h.update(f'{os.path.basename(name)}\0{len(data)}\0'.encode('utf-8', 'surrogateescape'))
        h.update(data)
    return h.hexdigest()


class Db(dict):
    """dpkg's database, {(package, architecture): fields}, with `digest`, db_digest of the bytes it was read from."""
    digest = None


def dpkg_db(P, files=None):
    """dpkg's database as dpkg itself reads it: the status file, then every entry of its journal,
    /var/lib/dpkg/updates, in order. dpkg writes each change to the journal first and folds the journal into
    the status file only at a checkpoint, so inside a dpkg run (in a maintainer script) the status file alone
    is out of date: on a first install, dpkg-stereo's postinst found packages configured earlier in the same
    run still "unpacked" there (ORDER 315). {(package, architecture): fields}; FILES, dpkg_db_files read once
    already, when the caller has them."""
    files = dpkg_db_files(P) if files is None else files
    db = Db()
    db.digest = db_digest(files)
    for _, data in files:
        with io.TextIOWrapper(io.BytesIO(data), encoding='utf-8', errors='surrogateescape') as f:
            for rec in _db_stanzas(f):
                if 'Package' in rec:
                    db[(rec['Package'], rec.get('Architecture', ''))] = rec
    return db


# The states whose files are the package version's own, configured or past its conffiles (dpkg(1), "Package states"):
# "installed"; "triggers-pending" (the package has been triggered) and "triggers-awaited" (it awaits another package's
# trigger processing), which a run with --no-triggers leaves until a later run processes them, as apt's runs leave
# systemd after any package ships a file in /usr/lib/systemd/catalog; and "half-configured" (configuration started:
# dpkg settled the conffiles when it left "unpacked", src/main/configure.c, and a package another one's unpack
# deconfigured, --auto-deconfigure, is in it until it is configured again). 0.1.28 and older counted only
# "installed": such a package looked removed, its records went, and its lean files stayed unrecorded (ORDER 367: on the
# lane's lean image 313 of systemd's 320 projected files after one `dpkg --no-triggers -i`).
INSTALLED_STATES = ('installed', 'triggers-pending', 'triggers-awaited', 'half-configured')


def _installed(P, db):
    """(name, fields) of each installed package in dpkg's database (INSTALLED_STATES), named as its file list is:
    package:arch for a Multi-Arch: same package or one whose list carries the architecture, the package alone
    otherwise."""
    try:
        lists = set(os.listdir(P.real('/var/lib/dpkg/info')))
    except FileNotFoundError:
        lists = set()
    for (pkg, arch), rec in (dpkg_db(P) if db is None else db).items():
        if rec.get('Status', '').rsplit(' ', 1)[-1] not in INSTALLED_STATES:
            continue
        name = pkg
        if rec.get('Multi-Arch') == 'same' or f'{pkg}:{arch}.list' in lists:
            name = f'{pkg}:{arch}'
        yield name, rec


class Installed(dict):
    """{package: (version, arch-qualified info name)}, with `digest`, the digest of the dpkg database they were read
    from (None when that is not known)."""
    digest = None


def dpkg_packages(P, db=None):
    """{package: (version, arch-qualified info name)} of the installed packages, from dpkg's database."""
    db = dpkg_db(P) if db is None else db
    pkgs = Installed((name, (rec.get('Version'), name)) for name, rec in _installed(P, db))
    pkgs.digest = getattr(db, 'digest', None)
    return pkgs


def twins(name, pkgs):
    """The other installed instances of a Multi-Arch: same package (libstdc++6:i386 for libstdc++6:amd64): they share
    every file both ship at one path, and Debian Policy 5.6.34.3 wants those "bit-identical"."""
    if ':' not in name:
        return []
    base = name.split(':')[0]
    return sorted(k for k in pkgs if k != name and ':' in k and k.split(':')[0] == base)


def twin_record(rec, name):
    """Whether REC, a projection record, is another instance's of the same package as NAME."""
    return bool(rec) and rec['package'] != name and ':' in name and rec['package'].split(':')[0] == name.split(':')[0]


def conffiles(P, db=None):
    """{package: {path: md5}}: each installed package's own conffiles as dpkg's database records them (the
    Conffiles field), named as dpkg_packages names the package. A path's hash is always asked of the package
    whose file list holds the path, never of another package that once had it: dpkg ignores "the 'conffiles'
    data in the status file ... when a package is not also listed in the file ownership database as having
    that file" (src/main/unpack.c). A conffile that moved leaves an entry in the package it left: on a fresh
    install binutils-common records /etc/gprofng.rc as "newconffile remove-on-upgrade" while binutils owns
    the file, and one map of every package's entries took that word for the file's hash (ORDER 325)."""
    return {name: rec.get('Conffiles', {}) for name, rec in _installed(P, db)}


def package_files(P, info):
    try:
        with open(P.real(f'/var/lib/dpkg/info/{info}.list'), encoding='utf-8', errors='surrogateescape') as f:
            return [l.rstrip('\n') for l in f if l.strip()]
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return []


def diversions(P):
    """dpkg diversions: a diverted path holds another package's file; such paths are left alone."""
    out = set()
    try:
        with open(P.real('/var/lib/dpkg/diversions'), encoding='utf-8', errors='surrogateescape') as f:
            lines = f.read().split('\n')
        for k in range(0, len(lines) - 2, 3):
            out.add(lines[k])
            out.add(lines[k + 1])
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass
    return out


def _work(args):
    path, real, package, seen = args
    try:
        st = os.lstat(real)
    except OSError:
        return path, None, 'missing', None
    if not os.path.isfile(real) or os.path.islink(real):
        return path, None, 'not a regular file', None
    if st.st_size == 0 or st.st_size > TEXT_SIZE_MAX:
        return path, None, 'empty' if st.st_size == 0 else 'larger than 4 MiB', None
    with open(real, 'rb') as f:
        data = f.read()
    if b'\0' in data[:8192]:
        return path, None, 'binary', None
    if seen and sha256(data) == seen[1]:
        return path, None, 'already projected', None
    if seen and sha256(data) == seen[0]:
        # these bytes were projected and checked before (the version dpkg replaced, or the same version
        # reinstalled): the same render gives the same lean bytes, and that pair needs no second check
        r = project.project(path, data, package.split(':')[0], check=False)
        if r.status == 'projected' and r.sha_out == seen[1]:
            r.check = 'the same bytes as a checked projection'
            return path, r, r.reason, data
    try:
        r = project.project(path, data, package.split(':')[0])
    except Exception as e:      # never let one file stop the whole run: it is left alone, with the reason
        r = project.Result(path=path, status='left', reason=f'adapter error: {type(e).__name__}: {e}'.split('\n')[0],
                           bytes_in=len(data), sha_in=project.sha256(data))
    return path, r, r.reason, data


OTHER_PYC = ('import py_compile, sys\n'
             'for a, c, o in zip(*[iter(sys.argv[1:])] * 3):\n'
             '    try:\n'
             '        py_compile.compile(a, cfile=c, doraise=True, optimize=int(o))\n'
             '        print("OK\\t" + c, flush=True)\n'
             '    except BaseException as e:\n'
             '        print("FAIL\\t" + c + "\\t" + (type(e).__name__ + ": " + str(e)).replace("\\n", " "), flush=True)\n')


def recompile_pyc(reals):
    """A projected .py keeps its mtime but not its size, so its byte-compiled files are stale: compile the
    ones that exist again, in place, with the same optimisation level, by the interpreter that made them.
    Returns what could not be refreshed, each {source, cache, interpreter, reason}: a compile error, a cache that
    cannot be written, an interpreter that is not installed or that failed (U20: these were dropped before). Python
    checks a cache against its source's size and time, so a stale cache is not run, only compiled again at each
    start where the cache folder is not writable."""
    import py_compile
    import importlib.util
    own = importlib.util.cache_from_source('x.py').rsplit('.', 2)[-2]      # e.g. cpython-314
    other = {}
    failed = []
    for real in reals:
        d, base = os.path.split(real)
        stem = base[:-3]
        cache = os.path.join(d, '__pycache__')
        if not os.path.isdir(cache):
            continue
        try:
            names = os.listdir(cache)
        except OSError as e:
            failed.append({'source': real, 'cache': cache, 'interpreter': own, 'reason': error_line(e)})
            continue
        for name in names:
            if not name.startswith(stem + '.') or not name.endswith('.pyc'):
                continue
            tag = name[len(stem) + 1:-4]
            opt = 2 if tag.endswith('.opt-2') else 1 if tag.endswith('.opt-1') else 0
            impl = tag.split('.')[0]
            if impl == own:
                try:
                    py_compile.compile(real, cfile=os.path.join(cache, name), doraise=True, optimize=opt)
                except Exception as e:      # PyCompileError, and OSError for a cache that cannot be written
                    failed.append({'source': real, 'cache': os.path.join(cache, name), 'interpreter': own,
                                   'reason': error_line(e)})
            else:
                other.setdefault(impl, []).append((real, os.path.join(cache, name), opt))
    for impl, items in other.items():
        version = impl.replace('cpython-', '')
        name = f'python{version[0]}.{version[1:]}'
        exe = shutil.which(name)
        if not exe:
            failed += [{'source': a, 'cache': c, 'interpreter': impl, 'reason': f'{name} is not installed'}
                       for a, c, _ in items]
            continue
        args = [x for it in items for x in (it[0], it[1], str(it[2]))]
        try:
            run = subprocess.run([exe, '-I', '-c', OTHER_PYC, *args], capture_output=True, text=True,
                                 errors='surrogateescape', timeout=900)
            out, rc, err = run.stdout, run.returncode, run.stderr
        except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as e:
            out, rc, err = '', None, error_line(e)
        done = set()
        for line in out.splitlines():
            parts = line.split('\t', 2)
            if parts[0] == 'OK' and len(parts) >= 2:
                done.add(parts[1])
            elif parts[0] == 'FAIL' and len(parts) == 3:
                done.add(parts[1])
                failed.append({'source': next((a for a, c, _ in items if c == parts[1]), ''), 'cache': parts[1],
                               'interpreter': impl, 'reason': parts[2]})
        for a, c, _ in items:
            if c not in done:
                tail = (err.strip().splitlines() or [''])[-1]
                failed.append({'source': a, 'cache': c, 'interpreter': impl,
                               'reason': f'{name} exited {rc}' + (f': {tail}' if tail else '')})
    return failed


def keep_bytecode(P, failed, replace=False):
    """The 'bytecode' problem: FAILED added to what is kept (or, with REPLACE, as all that is left)."""
    old = [] if replace else next((e.get('items', []) for e in kept_problems(P) if e.get('step') == 'bytecode'), [])
    items = {i['cache']: i for i in old + failed}
    if not items:
        return clear_problem(P, 'bytecode')
    items = [items[k] for k in sorted(items)]
    shown = '; '.join(f'{i["cache"]} ({i["interpreter"]}: {i["reason"]})' for i in items[:3])
    reason = ['LOCAL14', len(items), shown + (f' (+{len(items) - 3})' if len(items) > 3 else '')]
    return keep_problem(P, 'bytecode', reason, detail='\n'.join(f'{i["cache"]}\t{i["interpreter"]}\t{i["reason"]}'
                                                                 for i in items), items=items)


def write_lean(real, data, lean, st):
    tmp = real + '.dpkg-stereo-new'
    with open(tmp, 'wb') as f:
        f.write(lean)
    os.chown(tmp, st.st_uid, st.st_gid)
    os.chmod(tmp, st.st_mode & 0o7777)
    os.utime(tmp, ns=(st.st_atime_ns, st.st_mtime_ns))
    os.replace(tmp, real)


def store_pristine(P, data):
    d = P.state(PRISTINE)
    os.makedirs(d, exist_ok=True)
    p = os.path.join(d, sha256(data) + '.xz')
    if not os.path.exists(p):
        with lzma.open(p + '.new', 'wb', preset=9) as f:
            f.write(data)
        os.replace(p + '.new', p)


def load_pristine(P, sha):
    p = os.path.join(P.state(PRISTINE), sha + '.xz')
    try:
        with lzma.open(p) as f:
            return f.read()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return None


class Lock:
    def __init__(self, P):
        os.makedirs(P.state(), exist_ok=True)
        self.f = open(P.state('lock'), 'w')

    def __enter__(self):
        fcntl.flock(self.f, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
        return self

    def __exit__(self, *a):
        fcntl.flock(self.f, fcntl.LOCK_UN)
        self.f.close()


class _NoPool:
    def __enter__(self):
        return self

    def __exit__(self, *a):
        return False


PROGRESS_EVERY = 1000      # files between two progress lines of a long migration,
PROGRESS_SECONDS = 60      # and a line at least this often (some headers take GCC seconds each: ORDER 315 saw 8 minutes
                           # between two lines 1,000 files apart)


def project_packages(P, names, jobs, image=False, log=print, only=None, known=None, repair=True, record=True,
                     progress=None, db=None):
    """Project the installed files of these packages (every installed package without names). With `only`,
    just those paths. `record=False` leaves packages.tsv as it is: a migration inside a dpkg run must not mark
    the packages that run changed as seen, or post-invoke would never project them. `progress` gets a line
    every PROGRESS_EVERY files, and at least every PROGRESS_SECONDS. `db`: dpkg's database, when the caller read it
    already (post-invoke: a dpkg run is over when its hook runs, so it is the same)."""
    db = dpkg_db(P) if db is None else db
    if repair:
        repair_consumers(P, jobs, pkgs=dpkg_packages(P, db))
    pkgs = dpkg_packages(P, db)
    everything = pkgs
    if names:
        want = set(names)
        pkgs = {k: v for k, v in pkgs.items() if k in want or k.split(':')[0] in want}
    if only is not None:
        only = set(only)
    held = set(read_hold(P))
    # a path is judged by the conffiles and md5sums of the package whose file list holds it (dpkg -S), as
    # dpkg --verify checks each package's files against its own records (src/main/verify.c); another package
    # may still list the path from before it moved (a Replaces takeover takes the path out of the old
    # package's file list and leaves its md5sums as they were, src/main/unpack.c)
    confs = conffiles(P, db)
    div = diversions(P)
    # a shared file of a Multi-Arch: same package is recorded once, for the instance that projected it: the tables of
    # the other architectures' instances are read too, so their projection is seen (0.1.27, ORDER 363)
    tables = set(pkgs) | {t for k in pkgs for t in twins(k, everything)} if names else None
    files = Records(P, 'records', tables)
    left = Records(P, 'left', tables)
    tasks = []
    sums = {}
    built = lc_package.installed(P, pkgs)
    prebuilt = 0
    owned = None           # package -> its paths in each table, made once and only if an lc1 package needs it
    embedded = {}          # projected by an older engine, read as data by another file: path -> (package, reason)
    for name, (ver, info) in sorted(pkgs.items()):
        if name in held or name.split(':')[0] in held:
            continue
        proof = built.get(name)
        if isinstance(proof, dict):
            # These MR bytes ARE dpkg's baseline. Importing them into local records
            # would wrongly restore HR before an upgrade and provoke conffile prompts.
            if owned is None:
                owned = [{}, {}]
                for table, by in zip((files, left), owned):
                    for p, row in table.items():
                        by.setdefault(row['package'], []).append(p)
            for table, by in zip((files, left), owned):
                for path in by.get(name, ()):
                    del table[path]
            prebuilt += 1
            continue
        if proof is not None:
            log(f'dpkg-stereo: {name}: lc marker rejected ({proof}); using local qualification')
        sums[name] = md5sums(P, info)
        own = package_files(P, info)
        data_read = data_readers(P, own)
        for path in own:
            if path in held or path in div or (only is not None and path not in only):
                continue
            if path in data_read:
                # another file of the package reads it as data (Zig's @embedFile): it stays as authored
                if path in files and files[path]['package'] == name:
                    embedded[path] = (name, data_read[path])
                else:
                    left[path] = {'path': path, 'package': name, 'handler': '', 'reason': data_read[path]}
                continue
            rec = files.get(path)
            if rec and rec['version'] == ver and (rec['package'] == name or twin_record(rec, name)):
                # projected for this version, by this instance or by its other architecture's (a shared file, which
                # our dpkg replaced with the package's bytes when it unpacked this instance): the worker only looks
                # whether it is still lean (a file back in its package's bytes is projected again, a changed one is
                # left alone)
                tasks.append((path, P.real(path), name, (rec['sha_orig'], rec['sha_lean'])))
                continue
            tasks.append((path, P.real(path), name, (known or {}).get(path)))
    t0 = time.time()
    done = skipped = same = 0
    pys = []
    # a handful of files (a package or two after an upgrade) is quicker in this process than in a pool
    pool = None
    if len(tasks) > 200 and jobs > 1:
        from concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor
        pool = ProcessPoolExecutor(max_workers=jobs)
    results = pool.map(_work, tasks, chunksize=16) if pool else map(_work, tasks)
    if progress and tasks:
        progress(f'dpkg-stereo: projecting and checking {len(tasks)} files')
    said = time.time()
    with (pool or _NoPool()):
        for n, ((path, r, reason, data), (_, real, name, _seen)) in enumerate(zip(results, tasks), 1):
            if progress and n < len(tasks) and (n % PROGRESS_EVERY == 0 or time.time() - said >= PROGRESS_SECONDS):
                progress(f'dpkg-stereo: {n} of {len(tasks)} files projected and checked ({time.time() - t0:.0f} s)')
                said = time.time()
            if r is None:
                continue
            ver = pkgs[name][0]
            conf, own = confs.get(name, {}), sums.get(name, {})
            prior = files.get(path)
            if prior and prior['version'] == ver and (prior['package'] == name or twin_record(prior, name)) \
                    and sha256(data) != prior['sha_orig']:
                # projected for this version already (a shared file by its other architecture's instance, earlier in
                # this run or before): one projection, one record. A file changed after that projection keeps the
                # record, and --verify names it there, never as "changed since its package installed it" beside it
                # (ORDER 365: such a file was named twice, or not at all, by which second the hooks ran in)
                if left.get(path, {}).get('package') == name:
                    del left[path]
                continue
            if path not in conf and path in own and md5(data) != own[path]:
                left[path] = {'path': path, 'package': name, 'handler': r.handler,
                              'reason': 'changed since its package installed it (not the package\'s bytes)'}
                continue
            if path in conf and md5(data) != conf[path]:
                left[path] = {'path': path, 'package': name, 'handler': r.handler,
                              'reason': PERSON_CHANGED}
                continue
            if r.status != 'projected':
                if r.handler or r.reason not in ('no qualified adapter for this consumer',):
                    left[path] = {'path': path, 'package': name, 'handler': r.handler, 'reason': r.reason}
                else:
                    left.pop(path, None)     # an earlier reason no longer holds (a false "changed by the person")
                skipped += 1
                continue
            is_conf = path in conf
            if is_conf and md5(data) != conf[path]:
                left[path] = {'path': path, 'package': name, 'handler': r.handler,
                              'reason': PERSON_CHANGED}
                continue
            st = os.lstat(real)
            if is_conf:
                store_pristine(P, data)
                store_pristine(P, r.lean)
            write_lean(real, data, r.lean, st)
            if path.endswith('.py'):
                pys.append(real)
            files[path] = {'path': path, 'package': name, 'version': ver, 'conffile': int(is_conf),
                           'sha_orig': r.sha_in, 'md5_orig': md5(data), 'sha_lean': r.sha_out,
                           'handler': r.handler, 'variant': r.variant, 'bytes_orig': r.bytes_in, 'bytes_lean': r.bytes_out}
            left.pop(path, None)
            done += 1
            same += r.check == 'the same bytes as a checked projection'
    # the records first: a file written lean is recorded before anything else can fail
    files.save()
    left.save()
    if embedded:
        # their originals back (the engine that projected them did not know their reader), then left with the reason
        restore(P, set(embedded), pkgs={k for k, _ in embedded.values()}, log=log)
        kept = Records(P, 'records', {k for k, _ in embedded.values()})
        left = Records(P, 'left', {k for k, _ in embedded.values()})
        for path, (name, why) in embedded.items():
            if path not in kept:
                left[path] = {'path': path, 'package': name, 'handler': '', 'reason': why}
        left.save()
    failed = recompile_pyc(pys)
    if failed:
        keep_bytecode(P, failed)
        FAILED_STEPS.add('bytecode')
    if done or prebuilt:
        # An upgrade may introduce new settings or new authored text. The next
        # successful online update must refresh HR even on an existing system.
        try:
            os.unlink(P.state('hr-fetched'))
        except FileNotFoundError:
            pass
    if record:
        write_packages(P, everything)
    with open(P.state('identity'), 'w') as f:
        f.write(project.IDENTITY + '\n')
    log(f'dpkg-stereo: {done} files projected ({same} of them already checked in these bytes), {skipped} left '
        f'alone with their reason, {len(tasks)} examined, {prebuilt} lc1 packages already processed at build, '
        f'{time.time() - t0:.1f} s')
    return done


def data_readers(P, paths):
    """{path: reason} for the files of `paths` that another of them reads as data (lean.project.read_as_data)."""
    texts = {}
    for path in paths:
        if not project.reads_others(path):
            continue
        try:
            st = os.lstat(P.real(path))
            if os.path.isfile(P.real(path)) and not os.path.islink(P.real(path)) and 0 < st.st_size <= TEXT_SIZE_MAX:
                with open(P.real(path), 'rb') as f:
                    texts[path] = f.read()
        except OSError:
            continue
    return project.read_as_data(texts, paths) if texts else {}


def list_mtime(P, info):
    try:
        return str(int(os.stat(P.real(f'/var/lib/dpkg/info/{info}.list')).st_mtime))
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return ''


# packages.tsv's second header line: what dpkg's database and its file lists were when the table was written (0.1.29)
SEEN = '#seen'
SEEN_COLS = ('db', 'info', 'written', 'newest', 'rows')


def rows_digest(lines):
    return blake2b(''.join(lines).encode('utf-8', 'surrogateescape'), digest_size=16).hexdigest()


def info_mtime(P):
    """The time of dpkg's info directory, in nanoseconds: dpkg writes every file list through a new file renamed over
    the old one (lib/dpkg/db-fsys-files.c, write_filelist_except, with atomic_file_commit), and unpacks a package's
    control files into it the same way, so a list dpkg rewrote changes the directory's time."""
    try:
        return str(os.stat(P.real('/var/lib/dpkg/info')).st_mtime_ns)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return ''


def write_packages(P, pkgs=None):
    """packages.tsv: each installed package's version and the time of its file list, and in its second header line the
    digest of the dpkg database those rows come from (when PKGS carries it), the time of dpkg's info directory, the
    second it was written in, the newest file list's second and the digest of its rows."""
    pkgs = pkgs if pkgs is not None else dpkg_packages(P)
    rows = {k: {'package': k, 'version': v[0], 'list_mtime': list_mtime(P, v[1])} for k, v in pkgs.items()}
    info = info_mtime(P)
    written = str(int(time.time()))
    lines = []
    for k in sorted(rows):
        rows[k]['written'] = written
        lines.append('\t'.join(str(rows[k].get(c, '')) for c in PKG_COLS) + '\n')
    path = P.state(PKGS)
    with open(path + '.new', 'w', encoding='utf-8', errors='surrogateescape') as f:
        f.write('#' + '\t'.join(PKG_COLS) + '\n')
        digest = getattr(pkgs, 'digest', None)
        if digest:
            newest = max((int(r['list_mtime']) for r in rows.values() if r['list_mtime'].isdigit()), default=0)
            f.write('\t'.join((SEEN, digest, info, written, str(newest), rows_digest(lines))) + '\n')
        f.writelines(lines)
    os.replace(path + '.new', path)


def read_packages(P):
    """packages.tsv as write_packages left it: its rows, and what its second header line says was seen when they were
    written ({db, info, written, newest, rows}), or None when the table has no such line (0.1.28 and older wrote none)
    or its rows are no longer the ones it was written with."""
    rows, lines, seen = {}, [], None
    try:
        with open(P.state(PKGS), encoding='utf-8', errors='surrogateescape') as f:
            for line in f:
                if line.startswith(SEEN + '\t'):
                    seen = dict(zip(SEEN_COLS, line.rstrip('\n').split('\t')[1:]))
                    continue
                if line.startswith('#') or not line.strip():
                    continue
                lines.append(line)
                v = line.rstrip('\n').split('\t')
                rows[v[0]] = ({'package': v[0], 'version': v[1], 'list_mtime': v[2], 'written': v[3]} if len(v) == 4
                              else dict(zip(PKG_COLS, v)))
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass
    if seen is not None and seen.get('rows') != rows_digest(lines):
        seen = None
    return rows, seen


def as_seen(P, seen, digest):
    """Whether dpkg changed nothing since packages.tsv was written: its database holds the same bytes (so every
    package has the version and the state the rows record), its info directory has the same time (so no file list was
    written again: a reinstall of the same version leaves the database as it was, and rewrites the list), neither that
    time nor any row's list is in the second the table was written in (git's racy rule, as racy(): the newest list's
    second is in the line, and the rows are the ones it was written with)."""
    if not seen or not digest or seen.get('db') != digest or not seen.get('info') or seen['info'] != info_mtime(P):
        return False
    try:
        written = int(seen.get('written') or 0)
        return int(seen['info']) // 1000000000 < written and int(seen.get('newest') or written) < written
    except ValueError:
        return False


def size_may_be(real, size):
    """Whether the file at REAL may hold bytes of SIZE (a record's bytes_orig or bytes_lean): a file of another size
    cannot hash to them, so it is not read. A record without the size, and a file that cannot be looked at, may."""
    try:
        return os.stat(real).st_size == int(size)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return True
    except OSError:
        return True


def racy(row):
    """A file list dpkg wrote in the same second as the table that records it: a second rewrite in that
    second leaves the same time, so the package is looked at again (the rule git uses for its index)."""
    try:
        return int(row.get('written') or 0) > 0 and int(row.get('list_mtime') or 0) >= int(row['written'])
    except ValueError:
        return False


def md5sums(P, info):
    out = {}
    try:
        with open(P.real(f'/var/lib/dpkg/info/{info}.md5sums'), encoding='utf-8', errors='surrogateescape') as f:
            for line in f:
                h, _, rel = line.rstrip('\n').partition('  ')
                out['/' + rel] = h
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass
    return out


def read_hold(P):
    try:
        with open(P.state(HOLD), encoding='utf-8') as f:
            return [l.strip() for l in f if l.strip() and not l.startswith('#')]
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return []


def configurable(P, names):
    """Of NAMES, the packages dpkg's database does not show installed: the ones a configure run may configure. When the
    database holds the bytes packages.tsv was written from, its rows are the installed packages, and the database's
    fields are not read."""
    recorded, seen = read_packages(P)
    db_files = dpkg_db_files(P)
    if seen and seen.get('db') == db_digest(db_files):
        installed = recorded
    else:
        installed = dpkg_packages(P, dpkg_db(P, db_files))
    return {n for n in names if n not in installed}


def pre_invoke(P, action=None):
    """Projected conffiles still in their lean form go back to their pristine bytes, so dpkg finds them
    unmodified; a conffile the person edited (its bytes are no longer the lean ones) is left alone.
    dpkg runs this hook before it takes its lock, so a dpkg started while another one runs gets here, then
    stops at the lock and never runs post-invoke: the list for post-invoke is added to, never replaced,
    and what a stopped run restored is projected again by the next post-invoke.

    ACTION is dpkg's DPKG_HOOK_ACTION. A configure run compares the conffiles of the packages it configures, and only
    of those still unpacked (src/main/configure.c, deferred_configure: an installed package is "already installed and
    configured", a half-configured one had its conffiles settled before), so for `dpkg --configure` only the conffiles
    of the packages dpkg's database does not show installed go back (0.1.29, ORDER 367); an unpack or an install
    compares those of the packages it unpacks (src/main/unpack.c and archives.c, md5hash), which no hook is told, so
    every lean one goes back, as before."""
    _migrate(P)
    files = read_table(P.state(CONFFILES), FILE_COLS)
    only = configurable(P, {rec['package'] for rec in files.values()}) if action == 'configure' and files else None
    n = 0
    try:
        with open(P.state('restored-for-dpkg'), encoding='utf-8', errors='surrogateescape') as f:
            restored = [l.rstrip('\n') for l in f if l.strip()]
    except FileNotFoundError:
        restored = []
    listed = set(restored)
    for path, rec in files.items():
        if rec.get('conffile') != '1' or (only is not None and rec['package'] not in only):
            continue
        real = P.real(path)
        if not size_may_be(real, rec.get('bytes_lean')):
            continue                 # not the lean form's size: the person's edit, or already its package's bytes
        try:
            with open(real, 'rb') as f:
                data = f.read()
        except FileNotFoundError:
            continue
        if sha256(data) != rec['sha_lean']:
            continue
        orig = load_pristine(P, rec['sha_orig'])
        if orig is None or sha256(orig) != rec['sha_orig']:
            continue
        write_lean(real, data, orig, os.lstat(real))
        if path not in listed:
            restored.append(path)
            listed.add(path)
        n += 1
    tmp = P.state('restored-for-dpkg.new')
    with open(tmp, 'w', encoding='utf-8', errors='surrogateescape') as f:
        f.write(''.join(p + '\n' for p in restored))
    os.replace(tmp, P.state('restored-for-dpkg'))
    return n


def post_invoke(P, jobs):
    """After dpkg: the packages it installed, upgraded or reinstalled (their version or their file list
    changed) are projected again; the conffiles pre-invoke restored for it, and any recorded conffile found
    in its package's bytes, are projected again (the same render as before, so no new check); records of
    removed packages go. Beyond the conffiles, nothing else is read.

    A run in which dpkg changed nothing (its database holds the bytes packages.tsv was written from, and no file list
    was written again: as_seen) reads neither the database's fields nor any package's file list: the rows of
    packages.tsv are the installed packages, and no package changed (0.1.29, ORDER 367)."""
    _migrate(P)
    recorded, seen = read_packages(P)
    db_files = dpkg_db_files(P)
    digest = db_digest(db_files)
    unchanged = as_seen(P, seen, digest)
    if unchanged:
        pkgs = Installed((k, (v.get('version'), k)) for k, v in recorded.items())
        pkgs.digest = digest
        db = None
    else:
        db = dpkg_db(P, db_files)
        pkgs = dpkg_packages(P, db)
    repair_consumers(P, jobs, pkgs=pkgs)
    changed = [] if unchanged else sorted(k for k, v in pkgs.items()
                                          if recorded.get(k, {}).get('version') != v[0]
                                          or recorded.get(k, {}).get('list_mtime') != list_mtime(P, v[1])
                                          or racy(recorded.get(k, {})))
    gone = set() if unchanged else {k for k in recorded if k not in pkgs}
    ch = set(changed)
    try:
        with open(P.state('restored-for-dpkg'), encoding='utf-8', errors='surrogateescape') as f:
            restored = [l.rstrip('\n') for l in f if l.strip()]
    except FileNotFoundError:
        restored = []
    conf_idx = read_table(P.state(CONFFILES), FILE_COLS)
    # a recorded conffile in its package's bytes was restored for some dpkg run and never projected again
    # (that run stopped before its post-invoke, or its list was lost): it is projected now, like the list
    listed = set(restored)
    for path, rec in conf_idx.items():
        if rec.get('conffile') != '1' or rec['package'] in ch or path in listed:
            continue
        if not size_may_be(P.real(path), rec.get('bytes_orig')):
            continue                 # not the original's size: the projection, or the person's edit
        try:
            with open(P.real(path), 'rb') as f:
                if sha256(f.read()) == rec['sha_orig']:
                    restored.append(path)
        except OSError:
            pass
    # a shared file of a Multi-Arch: same package may be recorded for the other architecture's instance: its tables are
    # read too, so what dpkg did to the shared files of a changed instance is seen whether or not its twin also looks
    # changed (the racy rule makes that a matter of which second dpkg and this hook wrote in, ORDER 365)
    twin_of = {t: k for k in sorted(ch) for t in twins(k, pkgs) if t not in ch}
    files = Records(P, 'records', set(ch) | gone | set(twin_of)
                    | {conf_idx[p]['package'] for p in restored if p in conf_idx})
    dirty = False
    again = set()          # paths dpkg wrote back with the package's bytes: project them again
    heirs = Heirs(P, pkgs)
    for path in [p for p, r in files.items() if r['package'] in gone]:
        # a shared file stays on disk while another architecture's instance of its package ships it (dpkg removes it
        # with the last one, src/main/remove.c): its projection's record goes to that instance (0.1.27)
        heir = heirs.find(path, files[path])
        if heir:
            files[path] = dict(files[path], package=heir)
        else:
            del files[path]
        dirty = True
    if gone:
        # what a removed package left alone, with its reason, goes with it: --verify named a removed instance's
        # conffile "changed by the person" until the same path was left again (ORDER 365)
        left_gone = Records(P, 'left', gone)
        for path in [p for p, r in left_gone.items() if r['package'] in gone]:
            del left_gone[path]
        left_gone.save()
    same_version = {k for k in ch if recorded.get(k, {}).get('version') == pkgs[k][0]}
    known = {}             # what was projected and checked before, for files that come back in the same bytes
    # each recorded path of a changed instance once: its own records, then the records its twin holds for the paths
    # its file list shares
    examine = {p: r['package'] for p, r in files.items() if r['package'] in ch}
    lists = {}
    for path, rec in files.items():
        owner = twin_of.get(rec['package'])
        if owner and path not in examine and rec['version'] == pkgs[owner][0]:
            if owner not in lists:
                lists[owner] = set(package_files(P, pkgs[owner][1]))
            if path in lists[owner]:
                examine[path] = owner
    sums = {}
    conf = None
    for path, owner in sorted(examine.items()):
        rec = files[path]
        known[path] = (rec['sha_orig'], rec['sha_lean'])
        if owner in same_version or rec['version'] == pkgs[owner][0]:
            # dpkg rewrote the package's file list (a reinstall), or the list only looks different (an
            # image unpacked elsewhere), or this record was made from the installed version after dpkg
            # installed it (a migration earlier in this hook, which leaves packages.tsv as it was): the files
            # themselves say which of them dpkg replaced
            try:
                with open(P.real(path), 'rb') as f:
                    data = f.read()
                cur = sha256(data)
            except FileNotFoundError:
                data = cur = None
            if cur == rec['sha_lean']:
                continue
            if cur is not None and cur != rec['sha_orig']:
                # neither the projection nor its original: dpkg wrote other bytes of this version (its md5sums, or
                # its conffile hash, say so), or the person changed the file after its projection; then the record
                # stays, so --verify names the file whichever second dpkg and this hook ran in (ORDER 365: the
                # record went when the racy rule made the package look changed, and --verify passed silently)
                if owner not in sums:
                    sums[owner] = md5sums(P, pkgs[owner][1])
                if conf is None:
                    conf = conffiles(P, db)
                if md5(data) not in (sums[owner].get(path), conf.get(owner, {}).get(path)):
                    continue
            if owner in same_version:
                again.add(path)      # (a package whose version changed is projected whole below)
        del files[path]
        dirty = True
    for path in restored:
        rec = files.get(path)
        if not rec:
            continue
        real = P.real(path)
        try:
            with open(real, 'rb') as f:
                data = f.read()
        except FileNotFoundError:
            del files[path]
            dirty = True
            continue
        h = sha256(data)
        if h == rec['sha_lean']:
            continue                 # lean already (an older list named it again)
        if h != rec['sha_orig']:
            del files[path]          # not the bytes restored: dpkg or the person changed it
            dirty = True
            continue
        lean = load_pristine(P, rec['sha_lean'])
        if lean is None or sha256(lean) != rec['sha_lean']:
            r = project.project(path, data, rec['package'].split(':')[0], check=False)
            lean = r.lean if r.status == 'projected' and r.sha_out == rec['sha_lean'] else None
        if lean is not None:
            write_lean(real, data, lean, os.lstat(real))
        else:
            del files[path]
            dirty = True
            ch.add(rec['package'])
    os.unlink(P.state('restored-for-dpkg')) if os.path.exists(P.state('restored-for-dpkg')) else None
    files.save()
    full = sorted(ch - same_version)
    # repair_consumers ran at the start of this hook: once per dpkg run is enough
    if full:
        project_packages(P, full, jobs, log=lambda m: None, known=known, repair=False, db=db)
    if again:
        project_packages(P, sorted(same_version), jobs, log=lambda m: None, only=again, known=known, repair=False,
                         db=db)
    if not full and not again and not unchanged:
        # what this run saw, so the next run that finds dpkg's database and lists as they are now reads neither
        write_packages(P, pkgs)


class Heirs:
    """Which installed instance of a Multi-Arch: same package takes over the record of a shared file whose recorded
    instance dpkg removed: one of its other architecture at the recorded version whose file list holds the path, whose
    md5sums give the recorded original's MD5 (when the record has it, 0.1.27 and later), and the file still the
    recorded projection. Each instance's file list and md5sums are read once."""

    def __init__(self, P, pkgs):
        self.P, self.pkgs, self.lists, self.sums = P, pkgs, {}, {}

    def find(self, path, rec):
        try:
            with open(self.P.real(path), 'rb') as f:
                if sha256(f.read()) != rec['sha_lean']:
                    return None
        except OSError:
            return None
        for twin in twins(rec['package'], self.pkgs):
            ver, info = self.pkgs[twin]
            if ver != rec['version']:
                continue
            if info not in self.lists:
                self.lists[info] = set(package_files(self.P, info))
                self.sums[info] = md5sums(self.P, info)
            if path not in self.lists[info]:
                continue
            if rec.get('md5_orig') and self.sums[info].get(path) != rec['md5_orig']:
                continue
            return twin
        return None


def cached_debs(P):
    """The names of the .deb files in APT's cache, sorted."""
    try:
        return sorted(n for n in os.listdir(P.real('/var/cache/apt/archives')) if n.endswith('.deb'))
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return []


def apt_quote(s, bad):
    """apt's QuoteString (apt-pkg/contrib/strutl.cc): the characters of BAD, '%', spaces, control characters and
    anything outside ASCII as %xx in lower-case hex."""
    out = []
    for byte in s.encode('utf-8', 'surrogateescape'):
        ch = chr(byte)
        out.append(f'%{byte:02x}' if ch in bad or ch == '%' or byte <= 0x20 or byte >= 0x7f else ch)
    return ''.join(out)


def apt_name(name, version, arch):
    """The name APT gives this package version's .deb in its cache (apt-pkg/acquire-item.cc, pkgAcqArchive: package,
    version and architecture each through QuoteString, so an epoch's colon is %3a)."""
    return f'{apt_quote(name, "_:")}_{apt_quote(version, "_:")}_{apt_quote(arch, "_:.")}.deb'


def pool_name(name, version, arch):
    """The .deb's own name in a Debian archive, where the version goes without its epoch (Debian Policy 3.2.2:
    "Epochs are not included in the names of the files that compose source packages, or in the filenames of binary
    packages")."""
    return f'{name}_{version.split(":", 1)[1] if ":" in version else version}_{arch}.deb'


def in_cache(debs, package, version, arch=None):
    """Whether APT's cache (the sorted names cached_debs gives) holds this package version's .deb: for ARCH (or the
    package's own :arch) the file of that architecture only, apt's own name; without one, any architecture's."""
    name, _, qualified = package.partition(':')
    arch = arch or qualified
    if arch:
        exact = apt_name(name, version, arch)
        i = bisect.bisect_left(debs, exact)
        return i < len(debs) and debs[i] == exact
    prefix = f"{name}_{version.replace(':', '%3a')}_"
    i = bisect.bisect_left(debs, prefix)
    return i < len(debs) and debs[i].startswith(prefix)


def apt_offers(P, packages):
    """[(name, version, source)] of what APT's lists offer for these packages, from one apt-cache call: `apt-cache
    madison` lists the versions the sources hold, never dpkg's own record of an installed one (one call for 38
    packages took 0.63 s in ORDER 315's probe, where each failing `apt-get download` took 0.71 s); SOURCE is the
    package index as apt-cache policy names it (URI suite/component architecture Packages). None when APT cannot be
    asked (another root, no apt-cache, an error)."""
    if P.root != '' or not packages or not shutil.which('apt-cache'):
        return None
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(['apt-cache', 'madison', *sorted(set(packages))], capture_output=True, text=True,
                              errors='surrogateescape', timeout=900, env=dict(os.environ, LC_ALL='C.UTF-8'))
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        return None
    out = []
    for line in proc.stdout.splitlines():
        fields = [x.strip() for x in line.split('|')]
        if len(fields) >= 3 and fields[0] and fields[1]:
            out.append((fields[0], fields[1], fields[2]))
    return out


def archive_versions(P, packages):
    """{(name, version)} of what APT's lists offer for these packages (apt_offers). None when APT cannot be asked:
    every download is then tried, as before."""
    lines = apt_offers(P, packages)
    return None if lines is None else {(name, version) for name, version, _ in lines}


def debian_origin(origin):
    """Whether a Release file's Origin is the Debian archive's (Debian, Debian Backports, Debian Ports): the archives
    snapshot.debian.org keeps."""
    return origin == 'Debian' or origin.startswith('Debian ')


class AptView:
    """What APT's own data say about where the package versions restore needs come from (ORDER 365). The sources
    each Packages index belongs to and its Release file's Origin come from `apt-cache policy`; which versions each
    source lists, from `apt-cache madison`; dpkg's database gives the installed instance's architecture and
    Maintainer. For one package version, `plan` says whether a configured source offers it (APT downloads it, as
    before), whether snapshot.debian.org is asked when neither APT's cache nor its sources gave the .deb, and the
    archive it comes from otherwise:
      - a source offers this exact version: snapshot is asked only when that source's Origin is Debian's;
      - the sources list other versions of the package only: snapshot is asked when one of them is Debian's; when
        the installed version's Maintainer is that of the versions of some sources only (deb-multimedia's builds of
        a package Debian also has), those sources decide;
      - no list holds the package any more (Debian dropped it, or it never came from a configured source, as a
        vendor's downloaded .deb): nothing in APT or dpkg says where it came from, so snapshot is asked once, by its
        exact name, version and architecture; Debian's archive keeps every version Debian published, and the
        checks prove the file. A version snapshot does not hold is then said, with the --deb way;
      - APT cannot be asked (no apt-cache, another root, an error): the download is tried, then snapshot."""

    def __init__(self, P, needed, origins=True):
        # needed: {(package, version): (arch, maintainer)}; without ORIGINS only which versions APT offers (madison)
        self.needed = needed
        self.origins = {}
        self.lines = apt_offers(P, [k[0] for k in needed]) if needed else None
        self.maint = {}
        if self.lines is None or not origins:
            return
        try:
            proc = subprocess.run(['apt-cache', 'policy'], capture_output=True, text=True, errors='surrogateescape',
                                  timeout=900, env=dict(os.environ, LC_ALL='C.UTF-8'))
            policy = proc.stdout if proc.returncode == 0 else ''
        except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
            policy = ''
        source = None
        for line in policy.splitlines():
            words = line.strip()
            m = re.match(r'^-?\d+\s+(\S.*)$', words)
            if m and not words.startswith(('release ', 'origin ')):
                source = m.group(1).strip()
                self.origins.setdefault(source, '')
            elif words.startswith('release ') and source is not None:
                fields = dict(kv.split('=', 1) for kv in words[len('release '):].split(',') if '=' in kv)
                self.origins[source] = fields.get('o', '')
        # the installed version's Maintainer decides between sources only where both Debian and another archive list
        # the package and none lists this version: one apt-cache show for those packages
        ask = set()
        for (package, version), (arch, maintainer) in needed.items():
            mine = self.listed(package, arch)
            origins = {self.origins.get(s, '') for _, s in mine}
            if mine and version not in {v for v, _ in mine} and maintainer and len(origins) > 1 \
                    and any(debian_origin(o) for o in origins):
                ask.add(package.split(':')[0])
        if ask:
            try:
                proc = subprocess.run(['apt-cache', 'show', *sorted(ask)], capture_output=True, text=True,
                                      errors='surrogateescape', timeout=900, env=dict(os.environ, LC_ALL='C.UTF-8'))
                show = proc.stdout
            except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
                show = ''
            rec = {}
            for line in show.splitlines() + ['']:
                if not line.strip():
                    if rec.get('Package') and rec.get('Version'):
                        self.maint[(rec['Package'], rec['Version'])] = rec.get('Maintainer', '')
                    rec = {}
                elif line[0] not in ' \t' and ':' in line:
                    key, _, value = line.partition(':')
                    rec[key] = value.strip()

    @staticmethod
    def arch_ok(source, arch):
        """Whether an index (URI suite/component ARCH Packages, or a flat source's DIR Packages) can hold ARCH."""
        words = source.split()
        if arch in (None, '', 'all') or len(words) < 2 or words[-1] != 'Packages' or '/' in words[-2] \
                or words[-2] == '.':
            return True
        return words[-2] == arch

    def listed(self, package, arch):
        name = package.split(':')[0]
        return [(v, s) for n, v, s in (self.lines or ()) if n == name and self.arch_ok(s, arch)]

    def label(self, sources):
        return ', '.join(sorted({self.origins.get(s) or s for s in sources}))

    def plan(self, package, version):
        """(offered, snapshot, archive): whether a configured source offers this version, whether snapshot is
        asked, and the archive named when it is not."""
        arch, maintainer = self.needed.get((package, version), (None, ''))
        if self.lines is None:
            return True, True, ''
        mine = self.listed(package, arch)
        exact = [s for v, s in mine if v == version]
        if exact:
            return True, any(debian_origin(self.origins.get(s, '')) for s in exact), self.label(exact)
        if not mine:
            return False, True, ''
        name = package.split(':')[0]
        same = [(v, s) for v, s in mine if maintainer and self.maint.get((name, v)) == maintainer]
        decide = [s for _, s in (same or mine)]
        return False, any(debian_origin(self.origins.get(s, '')) for s in decide), self.label(decide)


def apt_download(url, target):
    """URL into TARGET through APT's own downloader, apt-helper download-file: APT's proxies, TLS and retries as
    apt.conf(5) sets them, as the sandbox user when root. None when the file arrived, else (missing, why): MISSING
    when the server answered that it has no such file (404, 410), WHY in APT's own words."""
    try:
        os.unlink(target)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass
    env = dict(os.environ, LC_ALL='C.UTF-8')
    env.pop('LANGUAGE', None)
    try:
        run = subprocess.run([APT_HELPER, 'download-file', url, target], capture_output=True, text=True,
                             errors='surrogateescape', env=env, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL)
    except OSError as e:
        return False, error_line(e)
    if run.returncode == 0 and os.path.isfile(target):
        return None
    out = (run.stderr + '\n' + run.stdout).splitlines()
    failed = [re.sub(r'^E: Failed to fetch \S+\s*', '', l.strip()) for l in out if l.startswith('E: Failed to fetch')]
    why = failed[0] if failed else ' '.join(l.strip() for l in out if l.startswith(('E:', 'W:'))) \
        or f'apt-helper exited {run.returncode}'
    status = re.match(r'(\d{3})\s', why)
    try:
        os.unlink(target)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        pass
    return bool(status and status.group(1) in ('404', '410')), why


def deb_fields(deb):
    """(Package, Version, Architecture) of a .deb's control file (dpkg-deb --show), or the reason it has none."""
    try:
        run = subprocess.run(['dpkg-deb', '--show', '--showformat=${Package}\t${Version}\t${Architecture}\n', deb],
                             capture_output=True, text=True, errors='surrogateescape')
    except OSError as e:
        return None, error_line(e)
    fields = run.stdout.rstrip('\n').split('\t')
    if run.returncode != 0 or len(fields) != 3:
        return None, ' '.join(run.stderr.strip().splitlines()[-2:]) or f'dpkg-deb --show exited {run.returncode}'
    return tuple(fields), None


def sha1_file(path):
    h = hashlib.sha1()
    with open(path, 'rb') as f:
        for block in iter(lambda: f.read(1 << 20), b''):
            h.update(block)
    return h.hexdigest()


def partial_dir(P):
    """APT's partial/ directory, made as APT makes it (the sandbox user _apt's, mode 700) when it is missing."""
    d = P.real('/var/cache/apt/archives/partial')
    if not os.path.isdir(d):
        os.makedirs(d, mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
        if os.geteuid() == 0:
            try:
                import pwd
                os.chown(d, pwd.getpwnam('_apt').pw_uid, 0)
            except (KeyError, OSError):
                pass
    return d


def snapshot_deb(P, name, version, arch):
    """The .deb of exactly NAME VERSION ARCH from snapshot.debian.org, fetched into APT's partial/ directory: the
    machine-readable interface names the file by its SHA-1, the file is fetched by that SHA-1, and it is kept only
    when its own SHA-1 is that one and its control file names this package, version and architecture. (path, None),
    or (None, (kind, why)): kind 'unreachable' (no answer, or an answer that is not the interface's), 'missing' (no
    such version for this architecture) or 'refused' (a file that is not the one asked for)."""
    q = lambda s: urllib_parse.quote(s, safe='')
    work = partial_dir(P)
    api = f'{SNAPSHOT}/mr/binary/{q(name)}/{q(version)}/binfiles?fileinfo=1'
    meta = os.path.join(work, f'dpkg-stereo.{os.getpid()}.json')
    got = apt_download(api, meta)
    if got is not None:
        return None, ('missing' if got[0] else 'unreachable', f'{api}: {got[1]}')
    try:
        with open(meta, encoding='utf-8') as f:
            info = json.load(f)
        results = info['result']
        hashes = list(dict.fromkeys(r['hash'] for r in results if r['architecture'] == arch))
    except (OSError, ValueError, TypeError, KeyError) as e:
        return None, ('unreachable', f'{api}: the answer is not the interface\'s ({error_line(e)})')
    finally:
        try:
            os.unlink(meta)
        except FileNotFoundError:
            pass
    if not hashes:
        others = sorted({str(r.get('architecture')) for r in results if isinstance(r, dict)})
        return None, ('missing', f'{api}: no {arch} file' + (f' (only {", ".join(others)})' if others else ''))
    target = os.path.join(work, f'dpkg-stereo.{os.getpid()}.{apt_name(name, version, arch)}')
    refused = []
    for sha in hashes:
        url = f'{SNAPSHOT}/file/{sha}'
        got = apt_download(url, target)
        if got is not None:
            if not got[0]:
                return None, ('unreachable', f'{url}: {got[1]}')
            refused.append(f'{url}: {got[1]}')
            continue
        have = sha1_file(target)
        fields, why = deb_fields(target)
        if have != sha:
            refused.append(f'{url} has SHA-1 {have}, not {sha}')
        elif fields != (name, version, arch):
            refused.append(f'{url} is ' + (' '.join(fields) if fields else f'no .deb ({why})'))
        else:
            return target, None
        os.unlink(target)
    return None, ('refused', '; '.join(refused))


def place_in_cache(P, path, name, version, arch):
    """A fetched .deb whose every check passed, moved from partial/ into APT's cache under APT's own name."""
    dest = os.path.join(P.real('/var/cache/apt/archives'), apt_name(name, version, arch))
    os.chmod(path, 0o644)
    if os.geteuid() == 0:
        os.chown(path, 0, 0)
    os.replace(path, dest)
    return dest


def apt_state(P):
    """What decides whether a missing .deb can come back, as two short digests: APT's lists (the name, size
    and time of every index in /var/lib/apt/lists) and the .deb files in APT's cache."""
    def digest(items):
        return hashlib.sha256('\n'.join(items).encode('utf-8', 'surrogateescape')).hexdigest()[:16]
    lists = []
    d = P.real('/var/lib/apt/lists')
    try:
        names = sorted(os.listdir(d))
    except FileNotFoundError:
        names = []
    for n in names:
        if n == 'lock':
            continue
        try:
            st = os.stat(os.path.join(d, n))
        except OSError:
            continue
        if stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode):
            lists.append(f'{n} {st.st_size} {st.st_mtime_ns}')
    return digest(lists), digest(cached_debs(P))


def deb_members(P, package, version, wanted, download=True, errors=None, arch=None, debs=None, why=None):
    """The pristine bytes of these files from the package's .deb, read in one pass of its archive: APT's
    cache first, then one download (unless `download` is false: APT's lists hold no such version); or only the .deb
    files DEBS names (one fetched from snapshot.debian.org, or one the person gave restore). With ARCH (or the
    package's own :arch) only that architecture's .deb in the cache is read, by APT's own name: a Multi-Arch twin's
    .deb of the other architecture holds other files. Files the archive does not hold are missing from the answer.
    When the archive could not be had or read, `errors` (a dict) gets (package, version) -> why: no .deb in the
    cache and none downloaded, apt-get download's own error, or dpkg-deb's or the archive reader's (U57: these were
    dropped, and a missing original looked like an archive that does not hold the file); `why` (a dict) gets the
    same as (kind, reason, file): kind 'root' (another root), 'unlisted' (no source offers it), 'apt' (the
    download failed) or 'unreadable' (FILE could not be read)."""
    name = package.split(':')[0]
    arch_q = package.split(':')[1] if ':' in package else None
    arch = arch or arch_q
    cands = []
    reasons = []
    cache = P.real('/var/cache/apt/archives')
    if debs is not None:
        cands = list(debs)
    elif os.path.isdir(cache):
        if arch:
            exact = os.path.join(cache, apt_name(name, version, arch))
            cands = [exact] if os.path.isfile(exact) else []
        else:
            ev = version.replace(':', '%3a')
            cands = [os.path.join(cache, f) for f in os.listdir(cache) if f.startswith(f'{name}_{ev}_')]
    tmp = None
    if not cands and debs is None:
        if P.root != '':
            reasons.append(('root', f'no .deb of {package} {version} in {cache} (another root is never downloaded into)',
                            None))
        elif not download:
            reasons.append(('unlisted', f'no .deb of {package} {version} in APT\'s cache, and APT\'s lists do not offer '
                            'it', None))
        else:
            tmp = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix='dpkg-stereo-')
            spec = f'{name}:{arch_q}={version}' if arch_q else f'{name}={version}'
            try:
                run = subprocess.run(['apt-get', 'download', spec], cwd=tmp, capture_output=True, text=True,
                                     errors='surrogateescape')
                rc, err = run.returncode, run.stderr
            except OSError as e:
                rc, err = None, error_line(e)
            cands = [os.path.join(tmp, f) for f in os.listdir(tmp) if f.endswith('.deb')]
            if rc != 0 or not cands:
                tail = ' '.join(l.strip() for l in err.strip().splitlines()[-2:])
                reasons.append(('apt', f'apt-get download {spec} ' + (f'exited {rc}' if rc != 0 else 'gave no .deb')
                                + (f': {tail}' if tail else ''), None))
    found = {}
    try:
        for deb in cands:
            proc = subprocess.Popen(['dpkg-deb', '--fsys-tarfile', deb], stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                                    stderr=subprocess.PIPE)
            complete = False
            problem = None
            try:
                with tarfile.open(fileobj=proc.stdout, mode='r|') as t:
                    for m in t:
                        path = m.name.lstrip('.')
                        if path in wanted and path not in found and m.isfile():
                            found[path] = t.extractfile(m).read()
                            if len(found) == len(wanted):
                                complete = True
                                break
            except (tarfile.TarError, OSError, EOFError) as e:
                problem = f'reading {os.path.basename(deb)}: {error_line(e)}'
            finally:
                proc.stdout.close()
                err = proc.stderr.read().decode('utf-8', 'replace')
                proc.stderr.close()
                rc = proc.wait()
            # a reader that stopped early, with everything it wanted, closes the pipe: dpkg-deb's error then is ours
            if not complete and (rc != 0 or problem):
                tail = ' '.join(l.strip() for l in err.strip().splitlines()[-2:])
                reasons.append(('unreadable', problem or f'dpkg-deb --fsys-tarfile {os.path.basename(deb)} exited {rc}'
                                + (f': {tail}' if tail else ''), deb))
            if len(found) == len(wanted):
                break
        if reasons and len(found) < len(wanted):
            if errors is not None:
                errors[(package, version)] = '; '.join(r[1] for r in reasons)
            if why is not None:
                why[(package, version)] = (reasons[0][0], '; '.join(r[1] for r in reasons), reasons[0][2])
        return found
    finally:
        if tmp:
            shutil.rmtree(tmp, ignore_errors=True)


def deb_member(P, package, version, path, errors=None):
    return deb_members(P, package, version, {path}, errors=errors).get(path)


def has_pristine(P, sha):
    return os.path.exists(os.path.join(P.state(PRISTINE), sha + '.xz'))


def original_bytes(P, rec, from_deb=None, errors=None):
    data = load_pristine(P, rec['sha_orig'])
    if data is None:
        data = from_deb.get(rec['path']) if from_deb is not None else \
            deb_member(P, rec['package'], rec['version'], rec['path'], errors=errors)
    if data is not None and sha256(data) == rec['sha_orig']:
        return data
    return None


def build_record(P, path):
    """The build's proof is separate from local records; it never changes dpkg baselines."""
    for info, obj in lc_package.installed(P).items():
        if not isinstance(obj, dict) or path not in package_files(P, info):
            continue
        for row in obj['files']:
            if row['path'] == path and row['status'] == 'projected':
                return obj, row
    return None, None


def build_original(P, obj, row, blobs=None, errors=None):
    path = lc_package.hr_path(obj['package'], obj['architecture'], row['sha_hr'])
    try:
        with lzma.open(P.real(path)) as stream:
            data = stream.read(TEXT_SIZE_MAX + 1)
    except (OSError, lzma.LZMAError):
        data = None
    if data is None:
        name = obj['hr_package']
        if obj['architecture'] != 'all':
            name += ':' + obj['architecture']
        blob = blobs.get(path) if blobs is not None else deb_member(P, name, obj['version'], path, errors=errors)
        if blob is not None:
            try:
                with lzma.open(io.BytesIO(blob)) as stream:
                    data = stream.read(TEXT_SIZE_MAX + 1)
            except (OSError, lzma.LZMAError):
                pass
    return data if data is not None and sha256(data) == row['sha_hr'] else None


def verify(P, names, deep=False):
    """Each projected file against its lean hash; its record against dpkg (the same package version, and
    for conffiles dpkg's own conffile hash and the pristine copy rendering to the installed lean bytes).
    With --deep, every other file's original is taken from its .deb (APT's cache) and checked against
    dpkg's md5sums and rendered again."""
    names = set(names or ())
    files = Records(P, 'records')
    db = dpkg_db(P)
    conf = conffiles(P, db)
    pkgs = dpkg_packages(P, db)
    bad = back = 0
    built_count = 0
    # --deep files whose original could not be had: never counted as checked (U57)
    unchecked = {}
    deb_errors = {}
    for name, obj in lc_package.installed(P, names or None).items():
        if names and name not in names and name.split(':')[0] not in names:
            continue
        if not isinstance(obj, dict):
            print(f'LCMARKER  {name} ({obj})')
            bad += 1
            continue
        built_count += 1
        for row in obj['files']:
            if not deep and row['status'] != 'projected':
                continue
            path = row['path']
            try:
                if row['kind'] == 'file':
                    good = not os.path.islink(P.real(path)) and lc_package.file_hash(P.real(path)) == row['sha256']
                else:
                    good = os.path.islink(P.real(path)) and os.readlink(P.real(path)) == row['target']
            except OSError:
                good = False
            if not good:
                print(f'LCCHANGED {path} (differs from its build-time payload; user edits remain untouched)')
                bad += 1
    sums_cache = {}
    # --deep reads each .deb once: the records go package by package, and one package's originals at a time
    items = sorted(files.items(), key=(lambda kv: (kv[1]['package'], kv[1]['version'], kv[0])) if deep else None)
    group, from_deb, wanted = None, {}, {}
    if deep:
        for p, r in files.items():
            if r.get('conffile') != '1':
                wanted.setdefault((r['package'], r['version']), set()).add(p)
    for path, rec in items:
        if names and rec['package'].split(':')[0] not in names and rec['package'] not in names:
            continue
        try:
            with open(P.real(path), 'rb') as f:
                data = f.read()
        except FileNotFoundError:
            print(f'MISSING   {path}')
            bad += 1
            continue
        h = sha256(data)
        if h != rec['sha_lean'] and h == rec['sha_orig']:
            print(f'RESTORED  {path} (its package\'s own bytes, not projected: the next dpkg run or '
                  f'dpkg-stereo --project projects it again)')
            back += 1
            continue
        if h != rec['sha_lean']:
            what = 'changed by the person' if rec.get('conffile') == '1' else 'changed since projection'
            print(f'CHANGED   {path} ({what})')
            bad += 1
            continue
        if pkgs.get(rec['package'], ('',))[0] != rec['version']:
            print(f'STALE     {path} ({rec["package"]} is no longer {rec["version"]})')
            bad += 1
            continue
        orig = None
        if rec.get('conffile') == '1':
            if conf.get(rec['package'], {}).get(path) != rec['md5_orig']:
                print(f'STALE     {path} (dpkg records another conffile version)')
                bad += 1
                continue
            orig = load_pristine(P, rec['sha_orig'])
            if orig is None or md5(orig) != rec['md5_orig']:
                print(f'PRISTINE  {path} (its pristine copy is missing or damaged)')
                bad += 1
                continue
        elif deep:
            info = pkgs[rec['package']][1]
            if info not in sums_cache:
                sums_cache[info] = md5sums(P, info)
            if group != (rec['package'], rec['version']):
                group = (rec['package'], rec['version'])
                from_deb = deb_members(P, rec['package'], rec['version'], wanted.get(group, set()), errors=deb_errors)
            orig = from_deb.get(path)
            if orig is None:
                why = deb_errors.get(group) or f'the .deb of {rec["package"]} {rec["version"]} does not hold it'
                print(f'NODEB     {path} (not checked against its package: {why})')
                unchecked.setdefault(group, [0, why])[0] += 1
                continue
            if md5(orig) != sums_cache[info].get(path) or sha256(orig) != rec['sha_orig']:
                print(f'DPKG      {path} (the package\'s bytes differ from the record or from dpkg\'s md5sums)')
                bad += 1
                continue
        if orig is not None:
            r = project.project(path, orig, rec['package'].split(':')[0], check=False)
            if r.sha_out != rec['sha_lean']:
                print(f'MISMATCH  {path} (the original does not render to the installed lean file)')
                bad += 1
                continue
    left = Records(P, 'left')
    for path, rec in sorted(left.items()):
        if names and rec['package'].split(':')[0] not in names and rec['package'] not in names:
            continue
        if 'changed by the person' in rec['reason']:
            print(f'EDITED    {path} (changed by the person: left as they made it)')
    # the C and C++ files whose original was at hand nowhere when the migration tried: checked above like any
    # other projection, in an older form (#line or every line kept: the authored line numbers), named here
    waiting = {}
    for path in pending_c_paths(P):
        rec = files.get(path)
        if rec and (not names or rec['package'] in names or rec['package'].split(':')[0] in names):
            waiting.setdefault((rec['package'], rec['version']), []).append(path)
    for (package, version), paths in sorted(waiting.items()):
        print(f'PENDING   {package} {version}: {len(paths)} C and C++ files keep an older engine\'s form (#line or '
              f'every line kept: their line numbers are the authored ones); no .deb of this version in APT\'s cache or lists. They are projected '
              f'again when it comes back, or when {package.split(":")[0]} is upgraded or reinstalled')
    lost = sum(n for n, _ in unchecked.values())
    print(f'dpkg-stereo: {len(files) - lost} projected files checked, {bad} problems'
          + (f', {back} in their package\'s own bytes (restored, not projected)' if back else '')
          + f', {built_count} lc1 package manifests checked'
          + (f', {sum(map(len, waiting.values()))} C and C++ files of {len(waiting)} package versions waiting for '
             f'their .deb (PENDING)' if waiting else '')
          + (f', {lost} files NOT checked against their package (NODEB)' if lost else ''))
    if lost:
        # an incomplete check is never a clean one: dpkg(1)'s exit status 2 is an error interacting with the system
        shown = '; '.join(f'{p} {v}: {why}' for (p, v), (_, why) in sorted(unchecked.items())[:3])
        say('LOCAL15', [lost, shown + (f' (+{len(unchecked) - 3})' if len(unchecked) > 3 else '')], sys.stdout)
    return 1 if bad else 2 if lost else 0


def tell(log, key, args=()):
    """KEY through LOG in the system's language, and in English too when that is another (as say() does)."""
    lang = language()
    log(f'{TAG}: {text(key, args, lang)}')
    if lang != 'en':
        log(f'{TAG}: (en) {text(key, args, "en")}')


def stays_lean(item, lang=None):
    """One package version whose files stay lean: how many, why, and the one thing that brings them back."""
    return text('LOCAL25', [item['package'], item['version'], item['files'], [WHY[item['why']]], item['detail'],
                            item['remedy']], lang)


def remedy(P, why, package, version, arch, offered, prerm, file=None):
    """What brings back the originals of a package version that stays lean for WHY ([KEY, ARG...]). Once
    dpkg-stereo is removed (its prerm runs the restore) dpkg-stereo cannot be asked again: a version a configured
    source offers comes back with apt-get's reinstall, any other with dpkg -i of exactly that .deb; apt-get's
    reinstall is never named for a version no configured source offers (it can only fetch what a source offers)."""
    name = package.split(':')[0]
    deb = pool_name(name, version, arch or 'all')
    if prerm and why != 'root':
        return ['LOCAL41', name] if offered and why in ('unreachable', 'apt', 'unreadable') else ['LOCAL42', deb, deb]
    if why == 'unreachable':
        return ['LOCAL35']
    if why == 'refused':
        return ['LOCAL37']
    if why == 'apt':
        return ['LOCAL38']
    if why == 'unreadable':
        return ['LOCAL39', file or deb]
    if why == 'root':
        return ['LOCAL40', P.real('/var/cache/apt/archives'), apt_name(name, version, arch or 'all')]
    if why == 'unlisted':
        return ['LOCAL51', package]
    return ['LOCAL36', deb, deb]


def restore(P, names, drop_hold=False, pkgs=None, log=print, progress=None, fetch=False, debs=()):
    """The original files back, records dropped: every record, or the packages and paths `names` lists.
    `pkgs` limits the records read to those packages (a caller that knows them); `progress` gets a line
    every PROGRESS_EVERY files, and at least every PROGRESS_SECONDS.

    An original without a pristine copy comes from its package version's .deb: APT's cache (that architecture's
    file, by APT's own name), then the configured sources (apt-get download), then, with FETCH (the person's
    --restore, --hold and --repair, and dpkg-stereo's own removal), snapshot.debian.org, for a package APT's data
    call Debian's or no longer know at all (AptView). A fetched .deb is checked three times: its SHA-1 against the
    one snapshot gives, its control file's Package, Version and Architecture against the installed ones, and every
    file it gives back against the record's sha_orig; it waits in APT's partial/ directory and goes into APT's
    cache under APT's own name only when all of them pass (ORDER 365). DEBS are .deb files the person hands restore
    (--deb): each is taken only when its control file is exactly an installed version whose files are recorded,
    and every file it gives back is the recorded original; otherwise nothing is taken from it. Without NAMES, DEBS
    name the packages to restore. What stays lean is said package version by package version, with why and the
    one thing that brings it back (LOCAL25), and kept as the restore problem."""
    # a set: with the list the C and C++ migration passed, this test cost 68.9 s on the station's 90,217 records
    # and 47,360 C and C++ names (ORDER 310), 0.05 s with a set
    names = set(names or ())
    note = progress or log
    db = {}

    def instance(package):
        """dpkg's database stanza of the installed instance records name PACKAGE (package or package:arch)."""
        if not db:
            db.update(dpkg_db(P))
        name, _, arch = package.partition(':')
        if arch:
            return db.get((name, arch)) or {'Architecture': arch}
        found = [r for (n, _), r in db.items() if n == name]
        return ([r for r in found if r.get('Status', '').endswith(' installed')] or found or [{}])[0]

    def arch_of(package):
        return package.partition(':')[2] or instance(package).get('Architecture') or None

    files = Records(P, 'records', pkgs)
    groups = {}
    for path, rec in files.items():
        groups.setdefault((rec['package'], rec['version']), []).append(path)
    # the person's own .deb files (--deb): exactly an installed version with recorded files, or refused
    given, refused = {}, 0
    for deb in debs or ():
        fields, err = deb_fields(deb)
        if fields is None:
            tell(log, 'LOCAL46', [deb, err])
            refused += 1
            continue
        match = [k for k in groups if k[0].split(':')[0] == fields[0] and k[1] == fields[1]
                 and arch_of(k[0]) == fields[2]]
        if not match:
            have = sorted(f'{k[0]} {k[1]}' for k in groups if k[0].split(':')[0] == fields[0])
            inst = instance(fields[0]) if not have else {}
            tell(log, 'LOCAL47', [deb, *fields, 'lean files are recorded for ' + ', '.join(have) if have else
                                  f'installed: {inst["Version"]} {inst.get("Architecture", "")}, no lean files'
                                  if inst.get('Version') else 'not installed'])
            refused += 1
            continue
        given[match[0]] = os.path.abspath(deb)
    if debs and not names:
        names = {k[0] for k in given}
        if not names:
            return 1
    ok = fail = 0
    todo = {}
    for (package, version), paths in sorted(groups.items()):
        for path in sorted(paths):
            if names and package.split(':')[0] not in names and package not in names and path not in names:
                continue
            todo.setdefault((package, version), []).append(path)
    pys = []
    missing = {}
    deb_errors = {}
    # the package versions that need their .deb and are not in APT's cache: one apt-cache call says which of
    # them APT's lists still offer, and only those are downloaded; where the others come from is AptView's
    cached = cached_debs(P)
    need = [k for k, paths in todo.items() if k not in given and not in_cache(cached, k[0], k[1], arch_of(k[0]))
            and any(not has_pristine(P, files[p]['sha_orig']) for p in paths)]
    view = AptView(P, {k: (arch_of(k[0]), instance(k[0]).get('Maintainer', '')) for k in need}, origins=fetch) \
        if need else None
    plans = {k: view.plan(*k) for k in need} if view else {}
    offline = {k for k, plan in plans.items() if not plan[0]}
    total = sum(map(len, todo.values()))
    if progress and total:
        progress(f'dpkg-stereo: putting back the originals of {total} files of {len(todo)} package versions '
                 f'({len(offline)} of them in no APT list or cache)')
    asked = [k for k in offline if plans[k][1]] if fetch and P.root == '' else []
    if asked:
        note(f'dpkg-stereo: {len(asked)} package versions are in no APT source or cache: their .deb is asked of '
             f'snapshot.debian.org ({SNAPSHOT}), one at a time')
    unreachable = []
    prerm = os.environ.get('DPKG_MAINTSCRIPT_NAME') == 'prerm'

    def originals(package, version, arch, wanted):
        """The originals of WANTED, where they came from (None, or ('given'|'snapshot', file)), and, when the .deb
        could not be had, (why, detail, file)."""
        name = package.split(':')[0]
        if (package, version) in given:
            got = {}
            m = deb_members(P, package, version, wanted, arch=arch, debs=[given[(package, version)]], why=got)
            w = got.get((package, version))
            return m, ('given', given[(package, version)]), w and ('given', w[1], None)
        offered, ask, archive = plans.get((package, version), (True, True, ''))
        got = {}
        m = deb_members(P, package, version, wanted, download=(package, version) not in offline, errors=deb_errors,
                        arch=arch, why=got)
        w = got.get((package, version))
        if w is None or w[0] not in ('unlisted', 'apt'):
            return m, None, w and (w[0], w[1], w[2])
        # neither APT's cache nor a configured source gave the .deb
        if not fetch:
            return m, None, ('unlisted' if w[0] == 'unlisted' else 'apt', w[1], None)
        if not ask:
            return m, None, ('apt', w[1], None) if offered else \
                ('other-archive', f'{archive}; {w[1]}' if archive else w[1], None)
        if unreachable:
            return m, None, ('unreachable', f'snapshot.debian.org was not asked again after: {unreachable[0]}', None)
        path, err = snapshot_deb(P, name, version, arch or 'all')
        if path is None:
            kind, detail = err
            if kind == 'unreachable':
                unreachable.append(detail)
            if offered:
                detail = f'{w[1]}; {detail}'
            return m, None, ({'unreachable': 'unreachable', 'refused': 'refused'}.get(kind) or
                             ('apt' if offered else 'not-on-snapshot'), detail, None)
        note(f'dpkg-stereo: {package} {version}: its .deb fetched from snapshot.debian.org ({os.path.getsize(path)} '
             f'bytes; its SHA-1 and its Package, Version and Architecture checked)')
        got = {}
        m = deb_members(P, package, version, wanted, arch=arch, debs=[path], why=got)
        w = got.get((package, version))
        return m, ('snapshot', path), w and (w[0], w[1], w[2])

    t0 = said = time.time()
    seen = 0
    # package by package: the originals without a pristine copy come from one pass of the package's .deb
    for i, ((package, version), paths) in enumerate(sorted(todo.items()), 1):
        arch = arch_of(package)
        lean_now = {}
        for path in paths:
            rec = files[path]
            try:
                with open(P.real(path), 'rb') as f:
                    data = f.read()
            except FileNotFoundError:
                del files[path]
                continue
            if sha256(data) != rec['sha_lean']:
                del files[path]     # the person's own file now; nothing of ours to undo
                continue
            lean_now[path] = data
        wanted = {p for p in lean_now if not has_pristine(P, files[p]['sha_orig'])}
        from_deb, source, why = ({}, None, None)
        if wanted:
            from_deb, source, why = originals(package, version, arch, wanted)
            if source and source[0] == 'given':
                wrong = sorted(p for p in wanted if from_deb.get(p) is None or sha256(from_deb[p]) != files[p]['sha_orig'])
                if wrong:
                    # a .deb the person hands restore is taken whole or not at all
                    tell(log, 'LOCAL48', [source[1], package.split(':')[0], version, arch or '', len(wrong), wrong[0]])
                    why = ('given', f'{os.path.basename(source[1])}: {len(wrong)} of {len(wanted)} files are not the '
                                    f'recorded originals, the first {wrong[0]}', None)
                    from_deb = {}
        stuck = []
        for path, data in lean_now.items():
            rec = files[path]
            orig = original_bytes(P, rec, from_deb)
            if orig is None:
                stuck.append(path)
                fail += 1
                continue
            real = P.real(path)
            write_lean(real, data, orig, os.lstat(real))
            if path.endswith('.py'):
                pys.append(real)
            del files[path]
            ok += 1
        if stuck:
            first = stuck[0]
            more = f' (and {len(stuck) - 1} more files)' if len(stuck) > 1 else ''
            src = ' from snapshot.debian.org' if source and source[0] == 'snapshot' else \
                f' {os.path.basename(source[1])}' if source else ''
            if from_deb.get(first) is not None:
                why = ('other-bytes', f'{first}: its .deb{src} holds other bytes than the recorded original{more}', None)
            elif why is None:
                why = ('other-bytes', f'{first}: its .deb{src} does not hold it{more}' if source or first in wanted
                       else f'{first}: its pristine copy is not the recorded original{more}', None)
            # whether a configured source offers this version, as far as APT was asked (a version whose .deb was in the
            # cache was not asked about: apt-get's reinstall is then never named for it)
            offered = plans.get((package, version), (False,))[0]
            missing[(package, version)] = {
                'package': package, 'version': version, 'files': len(stuck), 'why': why[0], 'detail': why[1],
                'remedy': remedy(P, why[0], package, version, arch, offered, prerm, why[2])}
        if source and source[0] == 'snapshot':
            if stuck:
                os.unlink(source[1])
            else:
                kept_at = place_in_cache(P, source[1], package.split(':')[0], version, arch or 'all')
                note(f'dpkg-stereo: {package} {version}: every file checked; its .deb kept in APT\'s cache as {kept_at}')
        if source and source[0] == 'given' and not stuck:
            tell(log, 'LOCAL49', [len(lean_now), package, version, source[1]])
        if progress and seen + len(paths) < total and (seen // PROGRESS_EVERY != (seen + len(paths)) // PROGRESS_EVERY
                                                       or time.time() - said >= PROGRESS_SECONDS):
            progress(f'dpkg-stereo: originals: {seen + len(paths)} of {total} files, {i} of {len(todo)} package '
                     f'versions ({time.time() - t0:.0f} s)')
            said = time.time()
        seen += len(paths)
    files.save()
    failed = recompile_pyc(pys)
    if failed:
        keep_bytecode(P, failed)
        FAILED_STEPS.add('bytecode')
    for key in sorted(missing):
        # the original could not be had: the files stay lean (and recorded); the reason is the archive's, APT's or
        # snapshot's own, and the remedy one that can work
        tell(log, 'LOCAL25', [missing[key]['package'], key[1], missing[key]['files'], [WHY[missing[key]['why']]],
                              missing[key]['detail'], missing[key]['remedy']])
    tell(log, 'LOCAL45', [ok, fail])
    if log is print:
        # a person's --restore, or the removal of dpkg-stereo: what stays lean is kept with its reason (U19); a
        # restore of some packages keeps what an earlier restore kept for the others, while their files stay lean
        items = [missing[k] for k in sorted(missing)]
        before = next((e for e in kept_problems(P) if e.get('step') == 'restore'), None)
        if before and names:
            done = {(i['package'], i['version']) for i in items} | set(todo)
            old = [i for i in before.get('items', []) if isinstance(i, dict) and i.get('package')
                   and (i['package'], i.get('version')) not in done]
            left = Records(P, 'records', {i['package'] for i in old})
            still = {(r['package'], r['version']) for r in left.values()}
            items += [i for i in old if (i['package'], i.get('version')) in still]
        if items:
            stuck_files = sum(int(i.get('files') or 0) for i in items)
            new = [i for i in items if i.get('why') in WHY] or [None]
            first = new[0]
            summary = ['LOCAL44', stuck_files, first['package'], first['version'], '@' + WHY[first['why']]] \
                if len(items) == 1 and first else \
                ['LOCAL43', stuck_files, len(items), first['package'], first['version'], '@' + WHY[first['why']]] \
                if first else ['LOCAL12', stuck_files, f'{len(items)} package versions']
            # what this run could not put back is said; the packages an earlier run kept are kept again quietly
            keep_problem(P, 'restore', summary, detail='\n'.join(stays_lean(i, 'en') if i.get('why') in WHY else
                                                                 f'{i["package"]} {i.get("version", "")}' for i in items),
                         # dpkg-stereo's own removal: once it is gone, only apt-get or dpkg can bring them back
                         repair=('; '.join(dict.fromkeys(text(i['remedy'][0], i['remedy'][1:], 'en') for i in items
                                                         if i.get('remedy')))
                                 if prerm else REPAIR),
                         items=items, quiet=not missing)
            if missing:
                FAILED_STEPS.add('restore')
        elif before or not names:
            clear_problem(P, 'restore')
    # the note is for a person's --restore; a migration (its own log) skips reading every lc1 manifest
    if log is print and any(isinstance(obj, dict) and (not names or name in names or name.split(':')[0] in names)
                            for name, obj in lc_package.installed(P).items()):
        log('dpkg-stereo: lc1 build-time files remain at their distribution baseline; --hr retrieves their authored text')
    return 1 if fail or refused else 0


def repair_consumers(P, jobs=4, pkgs=None, force=False):
    """The migrations of earlier local projections: GLSL shaders back to their authored bytes, then C and
    C++ files projected again in the current form (comment-only lines removed, engine 0.1.20). Each one reads the small record
    index, not every installed payload, and a marker makes later hooks a single existence check. `pkgs`:
    the installed packages, when the caller has them. `force` (a person's --repair-consumers) tries every
    waiting C and C++ file again now; the dpkg hooks and the postinst try one only when its package, APT's
    lists or APT's cache changed since the last try."""
    rc = repair_glsl(P)
    if not os.environ.get('DPKG_MAINTSCRIPT_NAME'):
        # inside a maintainer script dpkg's run is half done: the post-invoke hook at its end does this
        rc = repair_conffile_owners(P, jobs, pkgs=pkgs) or rc
    rc = migrate_qualified(P, jobs, pkgs=pkgs) or rc
    rc = migrate_perl_quotes(P, jobs, pkgs=pkgs) or rc
    rc = repair_twin_rows(P) or rc
    return migrate_c_form(P, jobs, pkgs=pkgs, force=force) or rc


TWIN_MIGRATION = 'shared-twin-records-v1'


def repair_twin_rows(P):
    """One projection, one record, for the shared files of Multi-Arch: same packages (0.1.27, ORDER 363). 0.1.26 and
    older read only the tables of the packages a dpkg run changed, so a shared file could end up recorded in the tables
    of two architectures' instances, and the instance that came second in a run kept a left row for the projection its
    twin had written ("changed since its package installed it"). The second record and that row go; every file and its
    other records stay as they are. Reads the record and left tables once; a marker makes later hooks a single existence
    check."""
    marker = P.state(TWIN_MIGRATION)
    if os.path.exists(marker):
        return 0
    rec_dir, left_dir = P.state('records'), P.state('left')
    try:
        names = sorted(f[:-4] for f in os.listdir(rec_dir) if f.endswith('.tsv') and ':' in f)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        names = []
    by_base = collections.defaultdict(list)
    for name in names:
        by_base[name.split(':')[0]].append(name)
    dropped = rows = 0
    owner = {}
    for base, group in by_base.items():
        tables = {name: read_table(os.path.join(rec_dir, name + '.tsv'), FILE_COLS) for name in group}
        for name in group:
            for path in tables[name]:
                owner.setdefault(path, name)
        if len(group) < 2:
            continue
        for name in group[1:]:
            twice = [path for path in tables[name] if owner.get(path) != name and owner.get(path) in tables
                     and tables[owner[path]][path]['sha_lean'] == tables[name][path]['sha_lean']]
            if twice:
                for path in twice:
                    del tables[name][path]
                write_table(os.path.join(rec_dir, name + '.tsv'), FILE_COLS, tables[name])
                dropped += len(twice)
    for name in names:
        f = os.path.join(left_dir, name + '.tsv')
        left = read_table(f, LEFT_COLS)
        false = []
        for path, row in left.items():
            other = owner.get(path)
            if other and other != name and other.split(':')[0] == name.split(':')[0]:
                rec = read_table(os.path.join(rec_dir, other + '.tsv'), FILE_COLS).get(path)
                try:
                    with open(P.real(path), 'rb') as stream:
                        lean = rec is not None and sha256(stream.read()) == rec['sha_lean']
                except OSError:
                    lean = False
                if lean:
                    false.append(path)
        if false:
            for path in false:
                del left[path]
            if left:
                write_table(f, LEFT_COLS, left)
            else:
                os.unlink(f)
            rows += len(false)
    if dropped or rows:
        # the small conffile index pre-invoke reads follows the record tables
        idx = {}
        for name in os.listdir(rec_dir):
            if name.endswith('.tsv'):
                idx.update({k: v for k, v in read_table(os.path.join(rec_dir, name), FILE_COLS).items()
                            if str(v.get('conffile')) == '1'})
        write_table(P.state(CONFFILES), FILE_COLS, idx)
        print(f'dpkg-stereo: shared files of Multi-Arch: same packages: {dropped} second records and {rows} left rows of '
              f'a projection the other architecture\'s instance made removed (one projection, one record)')
    os.makedirs(P.state(), exist_ok=True)
    with open(marker + '.new', 'w', encoding='utf-8') as stream:
        stream.write('Shared files of Multi-Arch: same packages recorded once, for one instance.\n')
    os.replace(marker + '.new', marker)
    return 0


QUALIFIED_MIGRATION = 'renderer-migration-0124-v1'
# what earlier engines refused where lean 0.1.24 has a qualified reading (ORDER 352)
RECHECK = ('check failed: GCC could not read the authored file', 'check failed: perl could not compile the authored file')


def newly_qualified(path):
    """A file of a format lean 0.1.24 qualifies first: Zig, and assembler sources the C preprocessor reads."""
    return project.reads_others(path) or os.path.splitext(path)[1] in ('.S', '.sx')


def migrate_qualified(P, jobs=4, pkgs=None):
    """Lean 0.1.24 (ORDER 352) qualifies Zig, assembler sources (.S, .sx), and C-family files in the mode their
    consumer reads them in (GCC's assembler-with-cpp, or the token and directive stream where GCC refuses only
    directives in conditional groups other targets take). The files packages installed before it are examined
    once, as a fresh install examines them: the packages that own a Zig or assembler source, and those with a file
    left as "check failed" by GCC or perl. A file another Zig file reads with @embedFile stays as authored (its
    original back where an older engine projected it). Packages processed at build (lc1) keep their build-time
    form. A marker makes later hooks a single existence check."""
    marker = P.state(QUALIFIED_MIGRATION)
    if os.path.exists(marker):
        return 0
    db = dpkg_db(P)
    pkgs = pkgs if pkgs is not None else dpkg_packages(P, db)
    names = {name for name, (ver, info) in pkgs.items() if any(newly_qualified(path) for path in package_files(P, info))}
    names |= {rec['package'] for rec in Records(P, 'left').values()
              if rec['reason'].startswith(RECHECK) and rec['package'] in pkgs}
    if names:
        t0 = time.time()
        done = project_packages(P, sorted(names), jobs, log=lambda m: None, repair=False, record=False)
        print(f'dpkg-stereo: files lean {lean_pkg.VERSION} qualifies first: {len(names)} packages examined, {done} '
              f'files projected; {time.time() - t0:.0f} s')
    os.makedirs(P.state(), exist_ok=True)
    with open(marker + '.new', 'w', encoding='utf-8') as stream:
        stream.write('Zig, assembler sources and earlier GCC and perl refusals examined with lean 0.1.24.\n')
    os.replace(marker + '.new', marker)
    return 0


PERL_QUOTES_MIGRATION = 'renderer-migration-perl-quotes-0125-v1'


def migrate_perl_quotes(P, jobs=4, pkgs=None):
    """Recheck the Perl files older renderers explicitly refused after stripping quote contents.

    Already verified projections and lc1 baselines remain valid. The ordinary projection path checks
    package checksums and holds, so an edited file stays the person's file. This is one examination of
    the recorded refusals, not a rescan of every installed package on a no-op dpkg run.
    """
    marker = P.state(PERL_QUOTES_MIGRATION)
    if os.path.exists(marker):
        return 0
    pkgs = pkgs if pkgs is not None else dpkg_packages(P)
    targets = {r['path']: r['package'] for r in Records(P, 'left').values()
               if r['handler'] == 'perl' and r['package'] in pkgs
               and r['reason'] == 'check failed: B::Deparse prints a different program'}
    if targets:
        done = project_packages(P, sorted(set(targets.values())), jobs, only=targets,
                                repair=False, record=False, log=lambda m: None)
        print(f'dpkg-stereo: Perl quote refusals rechecked with lean {lean_pkg.VERSION}: '
              f'{len(targets)} files examined, {done} projected')
    os.makedirs(P.state(), exist_ok=True)
    with open(marker + '.new', 'w', encoding='utf-8') as stream:
        stream.write('Recorded Perl quote refusals examined with lean 0.1.25.\n')
    os.replace(marker + '.new', marker)
    return 0


CONFFILE_MIGRATION = 'conffile-owner-v1'


def repair_conffile_owners(P, jobs=4, pkgs=None):
    """The conffiles dpkg-stereo 0.1.21 and older called "changed by the person" from a Conffiles entry dpkg
    does not use for them: one map of every package's entries gave a moved conffile the hash of the package it
    left (binutils-common's "newconffile remove-on-upgrade" for binutils' /etc/gprofng.rc, ORDER 325), and a
    path a package lists twice got its second entry's hash (nis's /etc/default/nis). Each such record is
    checked once against its own package's first entry: a file in the bytes that
    entry records, or one that package does not record as a conffile, was never the person's, so its record
    goes and the file is examined again like any other; a file whose bytes differ from its package's own
    hash stays the person's, as it was. Records of packages that are not installed stay as they are. Reads
    the small left tables once; a marker makes later hooks a single existence check."""
    marker = P.state(CONFFILE_MIGRATION)
    if os.path.exists(marker):
        return 0
    db = dpkg_db(P)
    pkgs = pkgs if pkgs is not None else dpkg_packages(P, db)
    confs = conffiles(P, db)
    left = Records(P, 'left')
    again = {}
    for path, rec in left.items():
        if rec['reason'] != PERSON_CHANGED or rec['package'] not in pkgs:
            continue
        try:
            with open(P.real(path), 'rb') as f:
                data = f.read()
        except OSError:
            continue
        own = confs.get(rec['package'], {}).get(path)
        if own is None or md5(data) == own:
            again[path] = rec['package']
    if again:
        for path in again:
            del left[path]
        left.save()
        print(f'dpkg-stereo: {len(again)} conffiles in their package\'s own bytes were recorded as changed by the '
              f'person (from a Conffiles entry dpkg does not use for them); examined again')
        project_packages(P, sorted(set(again.values())), jobs, log=lambda m: None, only=set(again), repair=False,
                         record=False)
    os.makedirs(P.state(), exist_ok=True)
    with open(marker + '.new', 'w', encoding='utf-8') as stream:
        stream.write('Conffiles recorded as changed by the person checked against their own package\'s hash.\n')
    os.replace(marker + '.new', marker)
    return 0


# v1 (dpkg-stereo 0.1.17): #line to kept line breaks; v2 (0.1.20): any older form to comment-only lines removed
C_MIGRATION = 'renderer-migration-c-drop-lines-v2'
C_MIGRATIONS_DONE = ('renderer-migration-c-line-breaks-v1',)     # earlier migrations' state, gone once v2 is done
PENDING_COLS = ('path', 'sha_lean', 'package', 'version')         # .pending: the files still in an older form
WAITING_COLS = ('package', 'version', 'files', 'lists', 'cache')   # .waiting: their versions, and APT's state when tried


def pending_c_paths(P):
    """{path: row} of the C and C++ files the migration still waits on (none once it is done)."""
    marker = P.state(C_MIGRATION)
    if os.path.exists(marker):
        return {}
    return read_table(marker + '.pending', PENDING_COLS)


def migrate_c_form(P, jobs=4, pkgs=None, force=False):
    """Project again, in the current form (comment-only lines removed, engine 0.1.20), the C and C++ files an
    earlier renderer projected in another: `#line` directives after every removed run (lean 0.1.15 and
    older; GCC paid more for those directives than the comments cost, ORDER 303) or every authored line kept
    (0.1.17 to 0.1.19; GCC still read every emptied line, ORDER 334). Each such file gets its exact original
    back (its pristine copy, or its package's .deb at the recorded version), then the current renderer
    projects and checks it like any other file. A file the person changed is theirs: restore drops its
    record and the projection leaves it as it is.

    A file whose original is nowhere keeps its old form (its line numbers are still the authored ones) and
    its record; it is listed in .pending, and its package version in .waiting with APT's lists and cache as
    they were at that try (ORDER 315: on the station 38 versions, 13,239 files, are in no archive at all, and
    trying them at every dpkg run cost about 80 s per run). A later run tries a version again only when its
    package's installed version, APT's lists or APT's cache changed, or when `force` says so; until then a
    dpkg run reads one small table. An upgrade or reinstall projects the package in the new form anyway."""
    marker = P.state(C_MIGRATION)
    if os.path.exists(marker):
        return 0
    pending_file, waiting_file = marker + '.pending', marker + '.waiting'
    first = not os.path.exists(pending_file)
    lists, cache = apt_state(P)
    waiting = {} if first else read_table(waiting_file, WAITING_COLS)
    due = None             # None: every recorded C and C++ file (the first run) or every pending one (0.1.17's list)
    if waiting:
        if pkgs is None:
            pkgs = dpkg_packages(P)
        due = {k for k, w in waiting.items() if force or pkgs.get(k, (None,))[0] != w['version']
               or w['lists'] != lists or w['cache'] != cache}
        if not due:
            return 1       # nothing that could bring an original back has changed: no record is read
    t0 = time.time()
    pending = {} if first else read_table(pending_file, PENDING_COLS)
    retry = set()
    if due is not None:
        if pkgs is None:
            pkgs = dpkg_packages(P)
        # a version dpkg replaced or removed is post-invoke's: its files are projected from the new package
        gone = {k for k in due if pkgs.get(k, (None,))[0] != waiting[k]['version']}
        maybe = due - gone
        debs = cached_debs(P)
        need = [k for k in maybe if not in_cache(debs, k, waiting[k]['version'])]
        listed = archive_versions(P, need) if need else set()
        retry = {k for k in maybe if k not in need or listed is None
                 or (k.split(':')[0], waiting[k]['version']) in listed}
        files = Records(P, 'records', retry)
    else:
        gone, maybe = set(), set()
        files = Records(P, 'records')
    from lean.lang_c import GLSL_EXTS     # shaders are repair_glsl's: restored, never projected again
    old = {path: rec for path, rec in files.items()
           if rec['handler'] == 'c' and os.path.splitext(path)[1].lower() not in GLSL_EXTS
           and (first or pending.get(path, {}).get('sha_lean') == rec['sha_lean'])
           and (due is None or rec['package'] in retry)}
    stuck = {}
    done = 0
    quiet = lambda m: None
    say = print if due is None or old else quiet
    if old:
        versions = {(r['package'], r['version']) for r in old.values()}
        say(f'dpkg-stereo: projecting {len(old)} C and C++ files again ({len(versions)} package versions), '
            f'comment-only lines removed (lean {lean_pkg.VERSION})')
        names = {r['package'] for r in old.values()}
        restore(P, set(old), pkgs=names, log=quiet, progress=say)
        kept = Records(P, 'records', names)
        again = {path for path in old if path not in kept}
        stuck = {path: {'path': path, 'sha_lean': kept[path]['sha_lean'], 'package': kept[path]['package'],
                        'version': kept[path]['version']} for path in old if path in kept}
        if again:
            done = project_packages(P, sorted({old[path]['package'] for path in again}), jobs, log=quiet, only=again,
                                    repair=False, record=False, progress=say)
    # the files still waiting: those of versions not tried now, and those whose original was again nowhere
    keep = {path: row for path, row in pending.items()
            if due is not None and row.get('package') and row['package'] not in gone and row['package'] not in retry}
    keep.update(stuck)
    os.makedirs(P.state(), exist_ok=True)
    if keep:
        by = {}
        for row in keep.values():
            by.setdefault(row['package'], [row['version'], 0])[1] += 1
        table = {}
        for package, (version, n) in by.items():
            w = waiting.get(package)
            tried_now = due is None or package in maybe or w is None or w['version'] != version
            table[package] = {'package': package, 'version': version, 'files': n,
                              'lists': lists if tried_now else w['lists'], 'cache': cache if tried_now else w['cache']}
        if keep != pending:
            # .waiting is trusted only once .pending is complete: it goes first and comes back last, so a run
            # cut short in between leaves no .waiting, and the next run tries every pending file once (as 0.1.17)
            if os.path.exists(waiting_file):
                os.unlink(waiting_file)
            write_table(pending_file, PENDING_COLS, keep)
        write_table(waiting_file, WAITING_COLS, table)
        if old or due is None:
            say(f'dpkg-stereo: C and C++ files in the new form: {done}; {len(keep)} files of '
                f'{len(table)} package versions keep an older form until their .deb is at hand (dpkg-stereo --verify '
                f'lists them; tried again when the package, APT\'s lists or its cache change); {time.time() - t0:.0f} s')
        return 1
    with open(marker + '.new', 'w', encoding='utf-8') as stream:
        stream.write('C and C++ projections of older engines projected again with their comment-only lines removed.\n')
    os.replace(marker + '.new', marker)
    for f in (pending_file, waiting_file) + tuple(P.state(m + x) for m in C_MIGRATIONS_DONE
                                                  for x in ('', '.pending', '.waiting')):
        if os.path.exists(f):
            os.unlink(f)
    if old:
        say(f'dpkg-stereo: C and C++ files in the new form: {done}, none left in an older form; '
            f'{time.time() - t0:.0f} s')
    return 0


def repair_glsl(P):
    """Undo the old C-checker projections of GLSL, once their originals are available.

    This migration reads the small record index, not every installed payload. A
    successful marker makes later hooks a single existence check. Missing exact
    originals keep their records and retry next time; edited or upgraded files
    are handled by restore's byte checks and are never overwritten.
    """
    marker = P.state('consumer-migration-glsl-v1')
    if os.path.exists(marker):
        return 0
    from lean.lang_c import GLSL_EXTS
    files = Records(P, 'records')
    paths = [path for path, rec in files.items()
             if rec['handler'] == 'c' and os.path.splitext(path)[1].lower() in GLSL_EXTS]
    if paths:
        print(f'dpkg-stereo: restoring {len(paths)} old GLSL projections; shaders need their own checker')
        if restore(P, paths):
            return 1
    os.makedirs(P.state(), exist_ok=True)
    with open(marker + '.new', 'w', encoding='utf-8') as stream:
        stream.write('Old C-checker shader projections restored or superseded.\n')
    os.replace(marker + '.new', marker)
    return 0


def hr_copy(P, path):
    files = Records(P, 'records')
    rec = files.get(path)
    errors = {}
    if not rec:
        obj, row = build_record(P, path)
        if row is None:
            print(f'dpkg-stereo: {path} has no valid local or build-time projection record')
            return 1
        orig = build_original(P, obj, row, errors=errors)
        name = obj['package']
    else:
        orig = original_bytes(P, rec, errors=errors)
        name = rec['package'].split(':')[0]
    if orig is None:
        print(f'dpkg-stereo: no pristine copy of {path}' + (f' ({"; ".join(errors.values())})' if errors else ''))
        return 1
    dest = P.real(f'/usr/share/doc/{name}/hr{path}')
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(dest), exist_ok=True)
    with open(dest, 'wb') as f:
        f.write(orig)
    print(dest)
    return 0


def fetch_hr(P):
    """The full HR copies of every projected setting, under /usr/share/doc/PACKAGE/hr/. What cannot be written is
    kept as the fetch-hr problem with each copy's reason (U19: APT's update hook used to throw away this output)."""
    _migrate(P)
    files = read_table(P.state(CONFFILES), FILE_COLS)
    n = 0
    missing = []           # (path, why)
    for path, rec in files.items():
        if rec.get('conffile') != '1':
            continue
        dest = P.real(f'/usr/share/doc/{rec["package"].split(":")[0]}/hr{path}')
        if os.path.exists(dest):
            continue
        orig = load_pristine(P, rec['sha_orig'])
        if orig is None:
            missing.append((path, f'its pristine copy is missing from {P.state(PRISTINE)}'))
            continue
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(dest), exist_ok=True)
        with open(dest, 'wb') as f:
            f.write(orig)
        n += 1
    conf = conffiles(P)
    for info, obj in lc_package.installed(P).items():
        if not isinstance(obj, dict) or not obj['hr_package']:
            continue
        rows = [row for row in obj['files'] if row['status'] == 'projected' and row['path'] in conf.get(info, {})]
        pending = []
        for row in rows:
            dest = P.real(f'/usr/share/doc/{obj["package"]}/hr{row["path"]}')
            if not os.path.exists(dest) or lc_package.file_hash(dest) != row['sha_hr']:
                pending.append((row, dest))
        if not pending:
            continue
        name = obj['hr_package'] + (':' + obj['architecture'] if obj['architecture'] != 'all' else '')
        errors = {}
        blobs = deb_members(P, name, obj['version'], {
            lc_package.hr_path(obj['package'], obj['architecture'], row['sha_hr']) for row, _ in pending},
            errors=errors)
        for row, dest in pending:
            orig = build_original(P, obj, row, blobs)
            if orig is None:
                missing.append((row['path'], errors.get((name, obj['version']))
                                or f'{name} {obj["version"]} holds no copy with the recorded hash'))
                continue
            os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(dest), exist_ok=True)
            with open(dest, 'wb') as stream:
                stream.write(orig)
            n += 1
    if not missing:
        open(P.state('hr-fetched'), 'w').close()
    elif os.path.exists(P.state('hr-fetched')):
        os.unlink(P.state('hr-fetched'))
    print(f'dpkg-stereo: {n} HR copies of settings written under /usr/share/doc/*/hr/')
    if missing:
        print(f'dpkg-stereo: {len(missing)} HR copies unavailable; the next online update tries again')
        shown = '; '.join(f'{path} ({why})' for path, why in missing[:3])
        keep_problem(P, 'fetch-hr', ['LOCAL13', len(missing), shown + (f' (+{len(missing) - 3})'
                                                                       if len(missing) > 3 else '')],
                     detail='\n'.join(f'{path}\t{why}' for path, why in missing))
        FAILED_STEPS.add('fetch-hr')
        return 1
    clear_problem(P, 'fetch-hr')
    return 0


def older_c_form(P, path, obj):
    """True when this lean C or C++ file is in an older engine's form, which keeps the authored line numbers:
    #line after every removed run (0.1.15 and older) or every authored line kept (0.1.17 to 0.1.19). An lc1
    package's manifest names its engine; a local projection is older while the migration still lists it,
    or before the migration ran."""
    if obj is not None:
        return 'c-drop-lines' not in obj['renderer']['identity']
    return not os.path.exists(P.state(C_MIGRATION)) and (
        path in pending_c_paths(P) or not os.path.exists(P.state(C_MIGRATION) + '.pending'))


def resolve(P, spec, by_render=False):
    path, _, rest = spec.partition(':')
    line, _, col = rest.partition(':')
    loc = f'{path}:{line}' + (f':{col}' if col else '')
    files = Records(P, 'records')
    rec = files.get(path)
    obj = row = None
    if not rec:
        obj, row = build_record(P, path)
        if row is None:
            try:
                with open(P.real(path), 'rb') as stream:
                    marked = b'Lean Code:' in stream.read(8192)
            except OSError:
                marked = True
            if marked:
                print(f'{path}:{line} (unresolved: no valid projection record)')
                return 1
            print(f'{loc} (not projected: the location is already the authored one)')
            return 0
        rec = dict(row, package=obj['package'])
    is_c = rec['handler'] == 'c' and rec['variant'] in ('c', 'c++')
    older = is_c and older_c_form(P, path, obj)
    orig = build_original(P, obj, row) if obj is not None else original_bytes(P, rec)
    if orig is None:
        if older:
            print(f'{loc} (this C or C++ file is in an older engine\'s form, which keeps the authored line numbers; '
                  'the column is the lean file\'s: no authored copy at hand to map it)')
            return 0
        print('dpkg-stereo: unresolved: no pristine copy of the authored file')
        return 1
    with open(P.real(path), 'rb') as f:
        mr = f.read()
    package = rec['package'].split(':')[0]
    note = ''
    try:
        if by_render:
            sl, sc = project.resolve(orig, mr, path, package, int(line), int(col) if col else None)
        else:
            found = locate.locate(orig, mr, path, int(line), int(col) if col else None)
            sl, sc = found.line, found.col
            if found.how.startswith('order'):
                note = f' (by {found.how})'
    except project.StaleMr as e:
        if not older:
            print(f'dpkg-stereo: unresolved: {e}')
            return 1
        # an older engine's form (#line, or every line kept): its line numbers are the authored ones
        print(f'{loc} (C and C++ in an older engine\'s form keep the authored line numbers, so the column is the '
              'lean file\'s; dpkg-stereo --resolve finds it by content)')
        return 0
    except Exception as e:
        print(f'dpkg-stereo: unresolved: {e}')
        return 1
    hr = f'/usr/share/doc/{package}/hr{path}'
    print(f'{hr}:{sl}' + (f':{sc}' if sc else '') + f' [lean {loc}]{note}')
    return 0


def manifest(P, out, roots):
    """Every comment-bearing text file in the image, projected or not, with the reason."""
    files = Records(P, 'records')
    left = Records(P, 'left')
    owner = {}
    built = {row['path']: (obj, row) for obj in lc_package.installed(P).values() if isinstance(obj, dict)
             for row in obj['files'] if row['status'] == 'projected'}
    for name, (ver, info) in dpkg_packages(P).items():
        for p in package_files(P, info):
            owner.setdefault(p, name)
    rows = 0
    with open(out, 'w', encoding='utf-8', errors='surrogateescape') as f:
        f.write('path\tpackage\tformat\tbytes\tlean_bytes\tprojected\treason\n')
        for image_prefix in roots:
            base = P.real(image_prefix)
            for d, dirs, names in os.walk(base):
                dirs[:] = [x for x in dirs if not (image_prefix == '/' and d == base and x in ('proc', 'sys', 'dev', 'run', 'tmp'))]
                for n in names:
                    real = os.path.join(d, n)
                    path = image_prefix.rstrip('/') + real[len(base):]
                    if path in built:
                        obj, row = built[path]
                        f.write(f'{path}\t{obj["package"]}\t{row["handler"]}/{row["variant"]}\t{row["bytes_hr"]}\t'
                                f'{row["bytes"]}\tyes\tlc1: projected at build\n')
                        rows += 1
                        continue
                    if path in files:
                        rec = files[path]
                        f.write(f'{path}\t{rec["package"]}\t{rec["handler"]}/{rec["variant"]}\t{rec["bytes_orig"]}\t'
                                f'{rec["bytes_lean"]}\tyes\tprojected\n')
                        rows += 1
                        continue
                    if path in left:
                        rec = left[path]
                        f.write(f'{path}\t{rec["package"]}\t{rec["handler"]}\t{os.path.getsize(real)}\t\tno\t{rec["reason"]}\n')
                        rows += 1
                        continue
                    try:
                        st = os.lstat(real)
                    except OSError:
                        continue
                    if not os.path.isfile(real) or os.path.islink(real) or st.st_size == 0 or st.st_size > TEXT_SIZE_MAX:
                        continue
                    with open(real, 'rb') as g:
                        head = g.read(8192)
                    if b'\0' in head:
                        continue
                    key, variant, why = consumers.choose(path, head)
                    if key is None and why == 'no qualified adapter for this consumer':
                        continue      # not a known comment-bearing format
                    reason = why or ('not owned by a package (made in the image)' if path not in owner else 'not examined')
                    f.write(f'{path}\t{owner.get(path, "")}\t{key or ""}\t{st.st_size}\t\tno\t{reason}\n')
                    rows += 1
    return rows


def status(P):
    files = Records(P, 'records')
    left = Records(P, 'left')
    by = {}
    for rec in files.values():
        k = rec['handler'] + ('/' + rec['variant'] if rec['variant'] else '')
        t = by.setdefault(k, [0, 0, 0])
        t[0] += 1
        t[1] += int(rec['bytes_orig'])
        t[2] += int(rec['bytes_lean'])
    # the version is the lean toolkit's, which this package carries (lean/__init__.py), not dpkg-stereo's own
    print(f'lean {lean_pkg.VERSION}: {len(files)} files projected, {len(left)} left alone, {len(read_hold(P))} held')
    for k, (n, a, b) in sorted(by.items(), key=lambda x: -(x[1][1] - x[1][2])):
        print(f'  {k:<20} {n:>7} files {a:>12} -> {b:>12} bytes ({a - b} fewer)')
    reasons = {}
    for rec in left.values():
        reasons[rec['reason']] = reasons.get(rec['reason'], 0) + 1
    for r, n in sorted(reasons.items(), key=lambda x: -x[1])[:20]:
        print(f'  left: {n:>7}  {r}')
    built = lc_package.installed(P)
    valid = [obj for obj in built.values() if isinstance(obj, dict)]
    projected = [row for obj in valid for row in obj['files'] if row['status'] == 'projected']
    print(f'  lc1: {len(valid)} packages processed at build, {len(projected)} projected files, '
          f'{sum(row["bytes_hr"] - row["bytes"] for row in projected)} gross payload bytes removed; '
          f'{len(built) - len(valid)} invalid markers')
    waiting = len(pending_c_paths(P))
    if waiting:
        print(f'  pending: {waiting} C and C++ files keep an older engine\'s form until their .deb is at hand '
              f'(dpkg-stereo --verify names them)')
    kept = kept_problems(P)
    print('  ' + (text('LOCAL18', [len(kept)]) + ' (dpkg-stereo --problems)' if kept else text('LOCAL17')))
    return 0


def show_problems(P):
    """Each kept problem: when, what did not complete and why, and what repairs it. Exit 1 while any is kept."""
    kept = kept_problems(P)
    if not kept:
        print(text('LOCAL17'))
        return 0
    lang = language(P)
    shown = 0
    for entry in kept:
        hidden = ''
        if entry.get('hidden'):
            e = engine(P)
            hidden = f' [{e.render("sparky-stereo-problems", "LOCAL24")["text"]}]' if e else ' [hidden]'
        else:
            shown += 1
        print(f'[{entry.get("time", "")}] {entry.get("step", "")}: {problem_text(entry, lang)}{hidden}')
        if lang != 'en' and not entry.get('unreadable'):
            print(f'  (en) {problem_text(entry, "en")}')
        # each package version whose files stay lean, with why and what brings it back (restore, ORDER 365)
        for item in entry.get('items', []):
            if isinstance(item, dict) and item.get('why') in WHY:
                print('  ' + stays_lean(item, lang))
                if lang != 'en':
                    print(f'    (en) {stays_lean(item, "en")}')
        if entry.get('repair'):
            print('  ' + text('LOCAL10', [entry['repair']], lang))
        print(f'  {entry["file"]}')
    return 1 if shown else 0


def repair(P, jobs):
    """Runs again what each kept problem names. A step that succeeds is cleared, one that fails again is kept with
    its new evidence. Exit 0 when nothing is left."""
    kept = {e.get('step'): e for e in kept_problems(P)}
    if not kept:
        print(text('LOCAL17'))
        return 0
    if P.root == '' and os.geteuid() != 0:
        say('LOCAL23', ['sudo dpkg-stereo --repair'])
        return 1
    left = 0
    for step, entry in sorted(kept.items(), key=lambda kv: kv[1].get('time', '')):
        FAILED_STEPS.clear()
        try:
            if step == 'bytecode':
                failed = recompile_pyc(sorted({i['source'] for i in entry.get('items', []) if i.get('source')}))
                keep_bytecode(P, failed, replace=True)
                ok = not failed
            elif step == 'fetch-hr':
                ok = fetch_hr(P) == 0
            elif step == 'restore':
                ok = restore(P, sorted({i['package'] for i in entry.get('items', [])}), fetch=True) == 0
            elif step in STEPS:
                # a hook, the projection or a migration: what dpkg changed since the last record, projected now
                repair_consumers(P, jobs, force=True)
                post_invoke(P, jobs)
                ok = not FAILED_STEPS
            else:
                ok = False
        except Exception as e:
            keep_problem(P, step if step in STEPS else 'project', error_line(e), detail=error_detail(), quiet=True)
            ok = False
        if ok:
            clear_problem(P, step)
            print(text('LOCAL19', [[STEPS[step]]]))
        else:
            left += 1
            again = next((e for e in kept_problems(P) if e.get('step') == step), entry)
            print(text('LOCAL20', [[STEPS.get(step, 'LOCAL3')], problem_text(again)]))
    return 1 if left else 0


def dismiss(P, step):
    """The engine's Hide: the problem stays listed, hidden for you until it fails again."""
    found = [e.get('step') for e in kept_problems(P)]
    if step not in found:
        print(text('LOCAL21', [step, ', '.join(found) or '-']))
        return 1
    e = engine(P)
    try:
        e.hide(TAG, step, scope=e.MACHINE)
    except e.Failed as error:
        print(f'{TAG}: {error}', file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
        return 1
    print(text('LOCAL22', [[STEPS.get(step, 'LOCAL3')]]))
    return 0


def notify(P):
    """The session's notifier (sparky-stereo-problems-plasma) shows every kept problem: this starts it."""
    return subprocess.run(['systemctl', '--user', 'start', 'sparky-stereo-problems-notifier.service']).returncode


def hook(P, pre, jobs=4):
    """dpkg's pre-invoke (PRE) or post-invoke hook. A hook never stops dpkg (dpkg ends its run with "cannot execute
    hook" when one fails): what goes wrong is said, kept with its evidence for --repair, and dpkg goes on with every file
    its package's original or a checked projection."""
    if os.environ.get('DPKG_STEREO_DISABLE'):
        return 0      # an image build turns the hooks off until its own Lean Code step
    step = 'pre-invoke' if pre else 'post-invoke'
    try:
        with Lock(P):
            if pre:
                # a removal or a purge removes conffiles without comparing them (src/main/remove.c has no
                # md5hash), and the others touch none
                action = os.environ.get('DPKG_HOOK_ACTION', '')
                if action not in ('triggers-only', 'remove', 'purge', 'add-architecture', 'remove-architecture'):
                    pre_invoke(P, action)
            else:
                post_invoke(P, jobs)
        clear_problem(P, step)
    except Exception as e:
        detail = error_detail()
        try:
            with open(P.state('error.log'), 'a', encoding='utf-8') as f:
                f.write(time.strftime('%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S ') + step + '\n' + detail)
        except OSError as error:
            print(f'{TAG}: {P.state("error.log")}: {error}', file=sys.stderr)
        keep_problem(P, step, error_line(e), detail=detail)
        say('LOCAL11')
    return 0


def main():
    if sys.argv[1:] in (['--pre-invoke'], ['--post-invoke']):
        # dpkg's hooks, exactly as /etc/dpkg/dpkg.cfg.d/dpkg-stereo runs them, at every dpkg run: no option to parse
        # (argparse alone took about 7 ms of a hook's start, ORDER 367)
        sys.stdout.reconfigure(line_buffering=True)
        return hook(Paths('/'), sys.argv[1] == '--pre-invoke')
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog='dpkg-stereo', description=__doc__.split('\n\n')[0])
    ap.add_argument('--root', default='/')
    ap.add_argument('--map', action='append', default=[], help='/etc=DIR: where an image folder really is')
    ap.add_argument('--jobs', type=int, default=4)
    ap.add_argument('--image', action='store_true')
    ap.add_argument('--if-changed', action='store_true',
                    help='with --repair-consumers: retry waiting files only when what decides it changed')
    g = ap.add_mutually_exclusive_group(required=True)
    g.add_argument('--project', nargs='*')
    g.add_argument('--pre-invoke', action='store_true')
    g.add_argument('--post-invoke', action='store_true')
    g.add_argument('--verify', nargs='*')
    ap.add_argument('--deep', action='store_true', help='with --verify: check every original against its .deb')
    g.add_argument('--restore', nargs='*')
    ap.add_argument('--deb', action='append', default=[], metavar='FILE.deb',
                    help='with --restore: a .deb of exactly an installed version, to take its originals from')
    g.add_argument('--repair-consumers', action='store_true')
    g.add_argument('--hold')
    g.add_argument('--unhold')
    g.add_argument('--hr')
    g.add_argument('--fetch-hr', action='store_true')
    g.add_argument('--resolve')
    g.add_argument('--resolve-render')
    g.add_argument('--manifest')
    g.add_argument('--status', action='store_true')
    g.add_argument('--problems', action='store_true')
    g.add_argument('--repair', action='store_true')
    g.add_argument('--dismiss')
    g.add_argument('--notify', action='store_true')
    a = ap.parse_args()
    if a.deb and a.restore is None:
        ap.error('--deb goes with --restore')
    # every line as it is written, even through a pipe: apt shows a long migration as it goes (ORDER 315)
    sys.stdout.reconfigure(line_buffering=True)
    P = Paths(a.root, [m.split('=', 1) for m in a.map])
    # the reading commands need no lock, and a person's session may run them
    if a.status:
        return status(P)
    if a.problems:
        return show_problems(P)
    if a.notify:
        return notify(P)
    if a.resolve:
        return resolve(P, a.resolve)
    if a.resolve_render:
        return resolve(P, a.resolve_render, by_render=True)
    if a.pre_invoke or a.post_invoke:
        return hook(P, a.pre_invoke, a.jobs)
    if a.dismiss:
        # the engine's Hide is the person's own: no lock, no administrator
        return dismiss(P, a.dismiss)
    # the commands that change the system: an error is said and kept (the step's name), and the command fails
    step = ('project' if a.project is not None else 'restore' if a.restore is not None or a.hold
            else 'repair-consumers' if a.repair_consumers else 'fetch-hr' if a.fetch_hr else None)
    try:
        with Lock(P):
            rc = run_command(P, a)
        # a full projection or the migrations, done: their problem is fixed (restore and fetch-hr clear their own)
        if (step == 'project' and not a.project or step == 'repair-consumers') and not FAILED_STEPS and rc == 0:
            clear_problem(P, step)
        return 1 if FAILED_STEPS and rc == 0 else rc
    except Exception as e:
        if step is None:
            raise
        keep_problem(P, step, error_line(e), detail=error_detail())
        return 1


def run_command(P, a):
    if a.project is not None:
        project_packages(P, a.project, a.jobs, a.image)
        return 0
    if a.verify is not None:
        return verify(P, a.verify, a.deep)
    if a.restore is not None:
        return restore(P, a.restore, fetch=True, debs=a.deb)
    if a.repair_consumers:
        return repair_consumers(P, a.jobs, force=not a.if_changed)
    if a.repair:
        return repair(P, a.jobs)
    if a.hold:
        rc = restore(P, [a.hold], fetch=True)
        with open(P.state(HOLD), 'a', encoding='utf-8') as f:
            f.write(a.hold + '\n')
        return rc
    if a.unhold:
        keep = [h for h in read_hold(P) if h != a.unhold]
        with open(P.state(HOLD), 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f:
            f.write(''.join(h + '\n' for h in keep))
        return 0
    if a.hr:
        return hr_copy(P, a.hr)
    if a.fetch_hr:
        return fetch_hr(P)
    if a.manifest:
        n = manifest(P, a.manifest, ['/'] if not P.maps else ['/usr', '/etc', '/var', '/opt', '/srv', '/root'])
        print(f'dpkg-stereo: manifest of {n} files written to {a.manifest}')
        return 0
    return 0


if __name__ == '__main__':
    sys.exit(main())
